## 2. The three layers

### [CXC-PC-LAYERS] Three layers: R runtime (exists, unchanged), P package manifest (new), H host (new)

- claim: a plug-in is described at three layers. R, the RUNTIME: `ComposePlugin` of spec 85, one typed pydantic declaration (Config plus publishes/subscribes) from which the config form, the announce and the registry entry are derived; it is UNCHANGED in v0.1, and its code ships in `composebus-v0.1.0`, so Gnomon obtains R by pinning composebus. P, the PACKAGE MANIFEST: the static, host-neutral `manifest.json` a host reads BEFORE any plug-in code runs; new in this spec. H, the HOST: consent (owned by the host), host sections, the shared management library, and the plain web Plugin Manager UI; new in this spec plus each host's own spec (Gnomon: spec 50, revised).
- rule: this spec MUST NOT change the meaning of anything in R. A change to R is a change to spec 85, not to this contract.
- why: what was missing is the layer you INSTALL and CONSENT to, plus a manager to drive it. The runtime half already exists.
- verify-machine: the conformance suite ([CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT]) runs against a `ComposePlugin` subclass imported from an UNMODIFIED `composebus-v0.1.0` install; a diff of `composeio/plugin.py` between the pinned tag and the contract release shows no contract-driven edit.
- verify-human: none required — structural.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER], [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]

### [CXC-PC-COMPOSEIO-HOST-IDENTITY] ComposeServer is ComposeIO's plug-in host; spec 13 "Service Hub v2" is not

- claim: ComposeServer (specs 85 and 87) is ComposeIO's plug-in host and serves the Plugin Manager UI. Spec 13 "Service Hub v2" is a pre-ComposeServer SMD and is NOT the host.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER], [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER]
- auth-token-is-hops-credential: The real operator token for the ComposeIO auth hook is a CREDENTIAL. Creating, storing and rotating it is HoP's call (security posture). No agent writes any secret into any secrets store. Builds and tests use a throwaway test token generated in scratch, which never becomes the real one. Until HoP provisions the real token, the production hook denies every request, because the key is absent.
- composeio-health-tick: (ComposeIO host only.) The ComposeIO host drives the degraded-reporting status tick from ComposeServer's per-tenant announce tick, so `health_reason()` is called on every tick at the in-process level as well as the subprocess level. A plug-in that becomes unwell after start is reported as degraded(<reason>) within one tick.
- composeio-auth-hook: (ComposeIO host only.) The auth hook uses HTTP Basic authentication, so that an operator can run the Plugin Manager from a plain browser, which prompts natively: username = operator name, password = that operator's token. It admits only loopback requests. Tokens are read BY REFERENCE from the operator's secrets file under the key `composeserver.plugin_manager.operators`, a mapping of operator name to token, and are compared in constant time. If the key or the file is absent, every request is denied, so the hook never defaults to open. No token is ever logged or echoed. The authenticated operator name becomes the consent record's `decided_by`. Provenance: architect-synthesis, composeux-architect 2026-11-26 (r15; Basic auth and the key shape set in r17, on the second-wave builder's finding that an HTML form cannot send a bearer header). ComposeServer has no HTTP auth of its own.

