## 3. Standing rules carried in verbatim

### [CXC-PC-RULE-BUS-OPTIONAL] The bus is available, never required

- rule: "The bus is available, never required. A Gnomon-only deployment always embeds the ComposeBus kernel, so any plug-in may use it. A plug-in that declares no topics needs no bus, and no plug-in depends on a separate ComposeIO install."
- consequence: a manifest with no `bus` block MUST install and run on a host with no bus attached to that plug-in; a plug-in package MUST NOT require ComposeIO to be installed (see [CXC-PC-LIBRARY]).
- verify-machine: a manifest with no `bus` key installs, enables and reaches `running` with the bus connector absent; the library's wheel metadata lists no dependency on a ComposeIO distribution (only `composebus` and the standard library).
- verify-human: none required.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIBRARY], [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK]

### [CXC-PC-RULE-TWO-HOST-TESTS] Two-host tests apply only to plug-ins that declare both hosts

- rule: "Two-host tests apply only to plug-ins that declare both hosts."
- consequence: a plug-in whose `hosts` declares one key is tested on that host only; hello-world declares both, so it runs on both ([CXC-PC-HELLO-V1]).
- verify-machine: the conformance runner enumerates hosts from the fixture's `hosts` keys; a fixture variant declaring only `gnomon` is NOT run on ComposeIO and the run reports that host as not-applicable, not as passed.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1], [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT]

### [CXC-PC-RULE-SIZE] v0.1 is the size the hello-world plug-in needs, no more

- rule: v0.1 is scoped to what the hello-world plug-in exercises, no more. A field that hello-world does not exercise is NOT in v0.1; its name is reserved ([CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]). The exceptions are the fields exercised by a dedicated schema test instead ([CXC-PC-STANDARD-QUESTIONS]).
- consequence: a builder MUST NOT add a manifest field because it seems useful; the only way a field enters is a new tagged contract release ([CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE]).
- verify-machine: every field of the shipped JSON Schema is exercised by a hello-world manifest (v1 carries `issue_tracker` and `codeowners`, so all optional fields except `standard_questions` appear) or by a negative control; `standard_questions` is exercised by the boundary tests of [CXC-PC-STANDARD-QUESTIONS] because hello-world need not ship one; a schema field exercised by none fails the schema-coverage check.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V2]
- v0-2-dedicated-tests: (0.2) `license` and `eula` are in contract 0.2 although hello-world does not exercise them. Like `standard_questions` and `permissions` they are exercised by dedicated tests instead (CXC-PC-V02-T-LICENSE-TABLE, CXC-PC-V02-T-EULA-FIELD-TABLE), so the schema-coverage check still holds. hello-world v1 and v2 are unchanged and their consent content is byte-identical to 0.1's apart from the page footer's contract version.

### [CXC-PC-RULE-REAL-TYPES] Bus declarations name real composebus-v0.1.0 types

- rule: "Bus declarations name real composebus-v0.1.0 types."
- consequence: `bus.publishes[].type` and `bus.subscribes[].type` MUST each name a member that exists in `composebus-v0.1.0`'s `MessageType`; the validator MUST refuse a name that does not exist, naming it. The further restriction to `STATE` and `EVENT` only is a separate architect-synthesis rule at [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK], not part of this rule.
- verify-machine: the validator, given `type: "FROBNICATE"`, refuses with the name and the list of valid names enumerated from the installed `composebus`; given `type: "ACK"` it refuses as reserved to kernel/host traffic; the eight reserved names are checked against `MessageType` members enumerated from the pinned `composebus` so a renamed member fails the check.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided — the brief's rule, in a comment HoP submitted
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK]

### [CXC-PC-RULE-CONSENT-HOST-OWNED] Consent is owned by the host, never by the plug-in

- consequence: no manifest field, plug-in code path or library default grants consent; see [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES].
- verify-machine: no manifest key can set `decision` or `granted`; an install driven with a host whose gate returns refuse leaves nothing installed (see negative control [CXC-PC-NC-WIDENING-REFUSED] for the upgrade case).
- verify-human: HoP, on the glass, starts an install from an agent session in Gnomon and confirms it WAITS for his decision.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES]

