## 4. Layer P: the package manifest

### [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FILE] The manifest is `manifest.json` at the package root; a JSON Schema ships with the library

- claim: the manifest is the file `manifest.json` at the plug-in package root. The file NAME and the JSON FORMAT are Home Assistant's. A JSON Schema for it ships with the contract library ([CXC-PC-LIBRARY-CONTENTS]).
- rule: a host MUST read and validate `manifest.json` WITHOUT importing or executing any plug-in code.
- boundary-case: a package with no `manifest.json` (at the directory root, or at `<import-package>/manifest.json` inside a wheel), or one that is not valid JSON, or that fails the schema, is REFUSED at install with the failing path named; nothing is written to the install root.
- verify-machine: install of a wheel lacking `manifest.json` refuses; a spy on `importlib.import_module` records ZERO imports of the plug-in's module during manifest read and consent rendering.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the architect's reading of HoP's expectation in gnomon:GM-0216 (a superset of the HA manifest); full provenance in the r23 revision record
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE], [CXC-PC-ADOPTED]
- v0-2-schema-files: (0.2) The library ships TWO JSON Schema files: `manifest-0.1.schema.json`, byte-identical to the file shipped with the 0.1.0 library, and a new `manifest-0.2.schema.json`. The schema that validates a manifest is the one selected by the manifest's own `contract` value (CXC-PC-V02-SCHEMA-FILES).

### [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] The v0.1 manifest fields — name, type, requiredness, default, source, meaning

| field | v0.1 | type and default | source | meaning |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `contract` | required | string `"MAJOR.MINOR"`, here `"0.1"` or `"0.2"`; no default | new | the contract version the manifest is written against; see [CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE] |
| `domain` | required | string, `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$`; no default | adopted-from-HA (integration manifest) | unique short id, never changes; also the plug-in's bus root (`plugins/<domain>/`) |
| `name` | required | string; no default | adopted-from-HA | display name |
| `version` | required | string, SemVer only | adopted-from-HA (custom integrations), narrowed | the plug-in's release version. HA also accepts CalVer via AwesomeVersion; v0.1 accepts SemVer ONLY |
| `description` | required | string, one line | adopted-from-HA-add-on | one-line purpose, shown at consent |
| `documentation` | required | string, `https://` URL only | adopted-from-HA, narrowed to https only | docs URL |
| `issue_tracker` | optional | string URL; absent by default | adopted-from-HA (custom integrations) | where users report problems |
| `codeowners` | optional | list of strings, each `@user` or `@org/team` (GitHub handle or team), matching `^@[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+(/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)?$`; absent by default | adopted-from-HA, HA's exact meaning and format; made optional | GitHub users or teams responsible for the plug-in; HA uses it to route code review. The consent screen shows it as "code owners", NOT as a verified publisher identity. HA requires it; v0.1 makes it optional |
| `requirements` | required (may be `[]`) | list of exact pip pins (`name==version`) | adopted-from-HA, narrowed (HA custom integrations allow any specifier; v0.1 allows only `==`) | shown at consent as "code this installs" |
| `run` | required | object `{form: "python", entry: "module:Class"}`; no default | new | how the plug-in is run. `entry` uses the Python entry-point object-reference syntax (adopted); the class MUST be a `ComposePlugin` subclass. See [CXC-PC-RUN] |
| `isolation_floor` | optional | `inprocess` or `subprocess`; default `inprocess` | new field; values and rule from spec 85 | the plug-in's isolation FLOOR. A plug-in may RAISE its floor; it may never force `inprocess` ([CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]) |
| `config_schema` | generated; ALWAYS present in a built manifest | JSON Schema of the plug-in's `Config` model (empty-object schema when there is no `Config`) | new; derived from spec 85 | written into the shipped manifest by the build command; see [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE] |
| `bus` | optional | object `{publishes: [...], subscribes: [...]}`; absent means no bus needed | new (type names are composebus-v0.1.0's) | the plug-in's bus declarations; see [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK] |
| `standard_questions` | optional | string, a relative path to a directory inside the package; absent by default (hello-world need not ship one) | new (the corpus itself is defined by gnomon:GM-RM-X145) | points to the plug-in's standard-questions corpus; its content and the CI check are defined by GM-RM-X145, not here; see [CXC-PC-STANDARD-QUESTIONS] |
| `hosts` | required | object with AT LEAST ONE key; no default | new | per-host sections; a key's presence means the host is supported; see [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS] |
| `license` | optional (v0.2) | string, an SPDX license expression, 1 to 256 ASCII characters, absent by default | new (v0.2, DEC-PLUGIN-EULA-IS-A-MANIFEST-CORE-FIELD-2026-11-27) | the plug-in's licence, informational: shown at consent, never verified, never part of a grant; see CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD |
| `eula` | optional (v0.2) | string, a package-relative path to one UTF-8 text file, absent by default | new (v0.2, DEC-PLUGIN-EULA-IS-A-MANIFEST-CORE-FIELD-2026-11-27) | the plug-in's end-user licence agreement, shown verbatim by any host that shows a EULA, its sha256 stored in the consent record; see CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD |

- required-fields: `contract`, `domain`, `name`, `version`, `description`, `documentation`, `requirements`, `run`, `hosts`
- optional-fields: `issue_tracker`, `codeowners`, `isolation_floor`, `bus`, `standard_questions`, `permissions` (r21, [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS]), `license` (v0.2, [CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD]), `eula` (v0.2, [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD])
- generated-fields: `config_schema`
- rule: the core of the manifest is EXACTLY the fields above; unknown core keys are REFUSED ([CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS]).
- boundary-case: `domain` failing `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$`, `version` not SemVer, `documentation` not `https://`, a `codeowners` entry that is not `@user` or `@org/team`, a `requirements` entry that is not an exact `==` pin or that names `composebus`, `compose-plugins` or `composeio`, a `run.entry` not of the form `module:object`, an `isolation_floor` outside the two values, a `hosts` object with zero keys, or a `standard_questions` path that is absolute, contains `..`, or does not exist inside the package, or a missing required field: each is a REFUSAL at install naming the field.
- verify-machine: a table-driven test supplies one manifest per boundary case above and asserts refusal naming exactly that field; a fully valid hello-world manifest passes; the schema-coverage check of [CXC-PC-RULE-SIZE] holds.
- verify-human: the consent screen for hello-world shows name, version, description, documentation, codeowners (when present) and requirements, each from the manifest alone, with `codeowners` labelled "code owners" and not presented as a verified publisher.
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided-by-architect — ratified by the architect 2026-11-26 (audit E6, r12), matching `validator-readings` on this node; HoP may overrule.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-ADOPTED], [CXC-PC-NOT-ADOPTED], [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES], [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR], [CXC-PC-STANDARD-QUESTIONS]
- validator-readings: NORMATIVE contract rules, so a second implementation cannot fork from the shared library: a manifest must validate identically in every host, and a rule kept only in library code is the fork these readings exist to prevent. The same holds for the PEP 503 comparison in `host-provided-packages` and for the consent-record string rules at [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD] `granted-format`. (1) `bus` carries BOTH `publishes` and `subscribes` when present; use [] for none. (2) Unknown keys inside a bus entry are refused. (3) `issue_tracker` is an http or https URL; `documentation` stays https only. (4) `name` and `description` are non-empty. (5) Each `requirements` entry is exactly `name==version`: no spaces, extras, markers or wildcards. (6) `run.entry` is `module:attr` with ASCII Python identifiers only. (7) `standard_questions` is a path relative to the directory holding manifest.json; it must be a directory, and may not be (or pass through) a symlink that leaves the package. Path names are compared EXACTLY and case-sensitively, on every filesystem: `SQ` does not match a directory named `sq` (G2). The same exact, case-sensitive matching applies to `manifest.json` itself, so a file named MANIFEST.JSON is refused (E1). A manifest.json that is a symlink is refused (N5). (8) The manifest reader refuses duplicate JSON keys and any non-finite number: NaN, Infinity, and a literal such as `1e999` (a finiteness check on parsed floats; G1). (9) In a wheel, the import package is the single top-level directory, other than *.dist-info and *.data, that holds a manifest.json; none or more than one is refused. (10) The consent screen renders only the INSTALLING host's own host section. (11) The consent-record store is append-only JSON lines under an exclusive file lock (macOS and Linux, consistent with the runner's platforms). (12) "Non-empty" means at least one character that is neither whitespace nor Unicode format (Cf). "One line" forbids every line and paragraph separator, including U+2028 and U+2029 (N3). Both rules also apply to the consent record's text fields (E2). The whitespace, Unicode-format and line-break character sets are frozen at Unicode 15.1, so the validator and schema agree whichever runtime evaluates them; the schema's ECMA reading assumes the `u` flag (E4). (13) Bus namespaces in a v0.1 manifest are exact topics: segments separated by single `/`, each segment matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]+`; no empty segments, no `.` or `..`, no leading or trailing `/`, no whitespace or control characters, and NO wildcards (`*`, `#`, `+`) in v0.1. Each must also pass composebus `validate_pattern` (G3). (14) manifest.json is at most 256 KiB, on disk or decompressed; nesting deeper than 64 levels is refused, and both refusals are named (never a raw RecursionError) (N1). (15) The shipped JSON Schema must agree with the validator under BOTH ECMA-262 and Python `re` semantics: anchor patterns with `(?![\s\S])` instead of `$`, and write `[0-9]` instead of `\d`. A test runs the adversarial set through Python `jsonschema` and asserts zero disagreements, apart from the rules the schema cannot express, which are listed in its description (S1). Single source: every string rule is one pattern in the validator, the shipped JSON Schema is generated from those patterns, and a test fails if the shipped file drifts from the generated one (E3).
- single-source-exceptions: (r22) `validator-readings` (15) says every string rule is one pattern in the validator from which the shipped JSON Schema is generated. One stated exception: the IPv6 RFC 5952 section 4 canonical form of a `permissions` network `target` ([CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS] `target-schema-coverage`) is a function, not one pattern; the validator alone enforces it, and the schema lists it in its description as a rule it cannot express, as (15) already allows for such rules.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) Two OPTIONAL fields are added, `capabilities` and `warnings`, valid only in a manifest that states contract "0.3" (CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES, CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS). The `contract` row now accepts "0.1", "0.2" and "0.3". The complete field set, frozen at 0.3, is the table at CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN. In a manifest that states "0.3" a grant-bearing `config_schema` property draws a named advisory, not a refusal (CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG). `required-fields` is unchanged: no existing field becomes required.

### [CXC-PC-STANDARD-QUESTIONS] An OPTIONAL `standard_questions` field points to the plug-in's standard-questions corpus; its content is defined by gnomon:GM-RM-X145, not here

- claim: v0.1 adds ONE optional manifest field, `standard_questions`, a path inside the package to the plug-in's standard-questions corpus. It is a pointer only: the corpus content, the fixed question set and the CI check are defined by gnomon:GM-RM-X145 ("Every plug-in ships a corpus answering a fixed set of standard questions, and CI checks it").
- clarification: these questions (what it does, when to use it, how to install it, how to call each verb) are asked by AGENTS at any time, NOT before install, and have nothing to do with consent.
- default: absent. hello-world need not ship one ([CXC-PC-HELLO-V1]).
- boundary-case: a `standard_questions` value that is absolute, contains `..`, or names a path that does not exist inside the package is REFUSED at install naming the path. The host does not read or check the corpus's content.
- verify-machine: a manifest with `standard_questions` pointing at an existing package directory installs; a manifest without it installs; an absolute path, a `..` path and a missing path are each refused naming the path; consent content ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT]) is identical with and without the field.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT], [CXC-PC-RULE-SIZE]

### [CXC-PC-RUN] `run` is `{form: "python", entry: "module:Class"}`; the class MUST be a `ComposePlugin` subclass

- claim: `run.form` is `python` in v0.1 (`process` and `container` are reserved, [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]). `run.entry` is a Python entry-point object reference: `module` or `module:object.attr`, here the form `module:Class`.
- rule: the class resolved from `run.entry` MUST be a `ComposePlugin` subclass; a resolved object that is not one is a refusal, never instantiated (the same rule as [CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE]'s resolved-class-must-subclass).
- default: none; `run` is required.
- boundary-case: `form` other than `python` is refused naming the value and that only `python` is valid in contract 0.1; an `entry` that does not import, or does not resolve, parks with a `resolve:` reason ([CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY]).
- verify-machine: a manifest with `form: "process"` is refused; an `entry` naming a plain class that is not a `ComposePlugin` subclass is refused without instantiation (a constructor side-effect canary is NOT triggered).
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — Python-only v0.1 was answered by gnomon-architect 2026-11-26 ("Python-only v0.1 is fine"); the entry syntax is adopted from packaging.python.org.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PC-ADOPTED], [CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE], [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY]
- import-package-name-rule: The plug-in's import package (the top-level module of `run.entry`, which is also the wheel's package directory) MUST NOT shadow a host-provided package (composebus, compose_plugins, composeio), a standard-library module (`sys.stdlib_module_names`), or any top-level module already loaded in the host process. Such a manifest is refused at install, naming the module. In-process enable MUST be atomic: if it fails for any reason, the host's `sys.path` and `sys.modules` are restored to exactly their state before the attempt. Reason: one kernel per host process; a plug-in package named like the kernel could otherwise replace the host's own modules even on a refused enable. Provenance: architect-synthesis, composeux-architect 2026-11-26 (r18), on independent QA FAIL 3; binds both hosts.

### [CXC-PC-BUS-BLOCK] `bus` declares publishes and subscribes as `{namespace, type}`, namespaces under `plugins/<domain>/`

- type-vocabulary: a plug-in's `bus` block may declare `type` `STATE` or `EVENT` ONLY, named exactly as composebus-v0.1.0's `MessageType` members (published as tag `composebus-v0.1.0`, public commit `7bad790`). The other members (`CONFIG_PUSH`, `CONFIG_REQUEST`, `HEARTBEAT`, `DISCOVERY`, `TIME_SYNC`, `BINDING`, `ACK`, `LOG_ENTRY`) are RESERVED to kernel/host traffic, and declaring one is REFUSED. Ruled architect-synthesis, ruled 2026-11-26 at ratify (pre-integration q9). Pointer (r24): compose-plugins now pins composebus 0.1.2 (published as tag `composebus-v0.1.2`, public commit `46327fb25ed3dbc4558971c67ef47a8e7422e562`), whose `MessageType` names are unchanged from 0.1.0 (no change in meaning).
- claim: `bus` is `{publishes: [{namespace, type}], subscribes: [{namespace, type}]}`. `type` is `STATE` or `EVENT` only (this restriction is this node's, architect-synthesis; the real-types rule is at [CXC-PC-RULE-REAL-TYPES]). In v0.1 every `namespace` MUST sit under `plugins/<domain>/`. An absent `bus` means no bus is needed ([CXC-PC-RULE-BUS-OPTIONAL]).
- why: the block is named `bus`, not `mqtt`, because HA's `mqtt` key is a discovery-topic list, NOT a bus declaration.
- default: `bus` absent = no publishes and no subscribes.
- boundary-case: a namespace outside `plugins/<domain>/` is refused naming it; a duplicate `{namespace, type}` pair within one list is refused; `type: "HEARTBEAT"` (or any other reserved member) is refused naming the member and that it is reserved to kernel/host traffic; a `type` that is not a `MessageType` member at all is refused as unknown.
- verify-machine: hello-world v1 passes with its exact topics: publish STATE `plugins/hello_world/greeting`, publish EVENT `plugins/hello_world/pong` and subscribe EVENT `plugins/hello_world/ping` (v2 adds subscribe EVENT `plugins/hello_world/name`); a manifest declaring `_server/anything` or `plugins/other_domain/x` is refused; each of the eight reserved types is refused and `STATE`/`EVENT` pass.
- verify-human: the consent screen lists every publish and every subscribe, one line each.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the shape and the plugins/<domain>/ restriction are the architect's; type names are composebus-v0.1.0's; full provenance in the r23 revision record
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-PROVING-ORDER], [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PC-RULE-REAL-TYPES], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]

### `permissions` declares network and filesystem access, declaration-only: shown at consent, recorded in the grant, never verified by any host [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS]

- claim: (r21) `permissions` is an OPTIONAL core field, `{network: [...], filesystem: [...]}`. It lets a plug-in declare the network and filesystem access it says it needs, so the manifest is structurally complete in v0.1. A declaration is shown on the consent screen and recorded in the consent record's `granted` list; it is never checked (`never-verified`).
- shape: `permissions` is an object whose ONLY keys are `network` and `filesystem`, each a list (either may be absent or `[]`). Any other key inside `permissions` is REFUSED at install, naming it. A network entry is exactly `{direction, target, purpose}`; a filesystem entry is exactly `{path, access, purpose}`; an unknown key inside an entry, or a missing one, is refused naming it (the same reading as bus entries, [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (2)).
- network-entry: `direction` is `outbound` or `inbound`. `target` is the endpoint: for `outbound` the remote endpoint the plug-in connects to, for `inbound` the endpoint it listens on. It is EXACTLY one of: the literal `any`; a host name (lowercase LDH labels `[a-z0-9-]`, 1 to 63 characters each, not starting or ending with `-`, separated by single `.`, at most 253 characters, no trailing `.`, and whose FINAL label is not all-numeric); an IPv4 address in canonical dotted-quad form (four decimal octets 0 to 255, no leading zeros); an IPv6 address in square brackets in the RFC 5952 section 4 form only (lowercase hex, leading zeros in each group suppressed, the longest run of two or more zero groups compressed with `::` (the first such run if two are equally long), no `::` for a single zero group); or any of those three followed by `:<port>`, a decimal port 1 to 65535 with no leading zeros. No wildcard (`*`, `?`, a leading `.`) and no `any:<port>`; `any` stands alone. (r22) A target made only of digits and dots is therefore valid only as a canonical dotted-quad IPv4 and is otherwise refused; the RFC 5952 section 5 mixed notation (for example `[::ffff:192.0.2.1]`) is REFUSED, and the plug-in writes `[::ffff:c000:201]`. "All-numeric" means the label matches `[0-9]+`; a known limit: hex-looking labels such as `0x7f` are accepted as host names. A non-ASCII (IDN) host name is refused by the character set; the plug-in writes its `xn--` form. A zone id (`%eth0`) is not in the grammar and is refused. Reason for the canonical forms: the grant strings are compared exactly for the widening rule ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING]), so these forms are not display-only and each address has one comparable spelling (`ipv6-refused-not-normalized`; schema coverage at `target-schema-coverage`).
- filesystem-entry: `access` is `read` or `read-write`. `path` is an absolute POSIX path: it starts with `/`, has no empty, `.` or `..` segment, no trailing `/` except the path `/` itself, no `~`, no wildcard (`*`, `?`, `[`), no NUL or control character, and is at most 4096 bytes in UTF-8. Paths are compared EXACTLY and case-sensitively, as in [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (7); the host does not resolve or look at the path.
- purpose-rule: `purpose` is required in every entry: non-empty and one line under [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (12), and at most 200 characters (Unicode code points). It is shown at consent beside the entry and is NOT part of the grant string, so changing it alone is not a widening ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING] `permissions-widen`).
- duplicates: Within `network`, two entries with the same `direction` and `target` are duplicates; within `filesystem`, two entries with the same `path` are duplicates, whatever their `access`. A duplicate is REFUSED at install, naming the entry. The manifest's size and nesting limits ([CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (14)) apply unchanged; there is no separate count limit.
- default: `permissions` absent means none declared, the same as `{network: [], filesystem: []}`; the consent screen then shows no permission lines.
- boundary-case: each is REFUSED at install naming the field or entry: a key in `permissions` other than `network` or `filesystem`; an entry with an unknown or missing key; a `direction` other than `outbound` or `inbound`; an `access` other than `read` or `read-write`; a `target` that is not `any`, a host name, a canonical IPv4 address or a bracketed section-4 IPv6 address, with an optional valid port (for example `*.example.com`, `any:443`, `Printer.Local`, `010.0.0.1` (a leading zero, so not canonical IPv4), `256.1.1.1` (octet over 255), `1.2.3` (all-numeric final label, not a quad), `printer.123` (all-numeric final label), `bücher.de` (non-ASCII; write `xn--bcher-kva.de`), `[::ffff:192.0.2.1]` (section 5 mixed notation), `[fe80::1%eth0]` (zone id), `[2001:db8:0:0:1::1]` (the second of two equal zero runs compressed), `printer.local:0`, `printer.local:65536`); a `path` that is relative, contains `..`, `.`, `//`, `~` or a wildcard, ends in `/`, or exceeds 4096 bytes; a `purpose` that is absent, blank, multi-line or over 200 characters; a duplicate entry (`duplicates`). ACCEPTED as targets: `printer.local`, `192.0.2.10`, `0.0.0.0`, `[::]`, `[::1]`, `[::ffff:c000:201]`, and `[2001:db8::1:0:0:1]` (the first of two equal zero runs compressed). A manifest with no `permissions`, or with both lists empty, installs.
- verify-machine: a table-driven test supplies one manifest per boundary case and asserts a refusal naming exactly that field or entry; (r22) in that table `010.0.0.1`, `256.1.1.1`, `1.2.3`, `printer.123`, `bücher.de`, `[::ffff:192.0.2.1]`, `[fe80::1%eth0]` and `[2001:db8:0:0:1::1]` are each refused naming `target`, and `printer.local`, `192.0.2.10`, `0.0.0.0`, `[::]`, `[::1]`, `[::ffff:c000:201]` and `[2001:db8::1:0:0:1]` are each accepted. Note for builders: Python's `ipaddress.IPv6Address(s).compressed == s` is NOT a correct implementation of the IPv6 rule, because it accepts a zone id and refuses `[::ffff:c000:201]` (it re-renders that address in mixed form); the table catches both. [CXC-PC-NC-MALFORMED-PERMISSION]; a valid manifest declaring `{direction: outbound, target: printer.local:631, purpose: ...}` and `{path: /tmp/hello, access: read-write, purpose: ...}` installs, its consent content shows both lines with their purposes, and its consent record's `granted` holds `permission.network:outbound:printer.local:631` and `permission.filesystem:read-write:/tmp/hello`; the shipped JSON Schema agrees with the validator on the same set ([CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (15)), apart from the IPv6 canonical-form cases it lists as inexpressible (`target-schema-coverage`); no host code path reads `permissions` except the validator, the consent renderer and the grant builder (checked by a test that runs a plug-in which uses the network without declaring it and asserts it is neither refused nor parked).
- verify-human: an operator reads a consent screen with one network and one filesystem declaration and can say, from the screen alone, what the plug-in says it needs and why.
- gnomon-host-section-network: (r21) The network key that gnomon-architect had added to Gnomon's host section in Gnomon spec 50 is SUPERSEDED for declaring network access by this core field, which is the one form: no second key for declaring network access exists. Confirmed by gnomon-architect, spec 50 r3.1, which dropped its key and reads the core field (as relayed by composeux-architect, 2026-11-26). This file does not edit spec 50.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING], [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES], [CXC-PC-NC-MALFORMED-PERMISSION]
- never-verified-scope: [A, composeux-architect:] A declaration is shown at consent and recorded in the grant. (i) The operator declining consent is NOT covered by `never-verified` and stays fully available, and a host still refuses a malformed declaration at install (`shape`, `boundary-case`). (ii) Any future use of declarations to restrict a plug-in (for example a v0.2 container run form configuring its network from them) would be a contract change put to HoP; `never-verified` neither forbids nor allows it by itself.
- target-schema-coverage: (r22) The shipped JSON Schema carries as patterns these `target` rules: the character set and label shape of a host name, the final label not being all-numeric, the dotted-quad IPv4 form with each octet 0 to 255 and no leading zeros, the bracket and character set of an IPv6 literal, and the port range 1 to 65535 with no leading zeros. It CANNOT carry the IPv6 RFC 5952 section 4 canonical form (lower case, leading zeros suppressed, the longest run of two or more zero groups compressed, the first run on a tie, no `::` for a single group): that is a function over the eight groups, not one pattern, so the VALIDATOR alone enforces it, and the schema lists it in its description as inexpressible ([CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (15), S1). This is the stated exception to (15)'s "every string rule is one pattern", recorded at [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `single-source-exceptions`.
- ipv6-refused-not-normalized: (r22) A non-canonical IPv6 literal is REFUSED, not normalized, because the purpose of the canonical form is one comparable string for the widening rule, and normalizing would need the same function anyway. The audit's alternative (accept any well-formed bracketed literal and compare exactly, at the cost of an extra consent prompt when a plug-in respells an address) was considered and not taken.

### [CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS] Unknown keys in the core are REFUSED; unknown keys inside ANOTHER host's section are ignored by design

- rule: a key at the top level of the manifest, or inside `run` or `bus`, that this contract version does not define MUST be REFUSED at install, naming the key (fail loud). Home Assistant's names are RESERVED ([CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]) and a manifest carrying any of them is REFUSED like any unknown key; there is no accept-and-ignore list and no install-time warning. A key inside a host's OWN section is validated by that host; a key inside ANOTHER host's section MUST be ignored; a key in `hosts` naming an unknown host is ignored too ([CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS]).
- why: HA add-ons silently ignore unknown keys. v0.1 rejects that, because a mistyped permission field must never pass silently. HA's names are reserved rather than tolerated: v0.1 is not HA-manifest-compatible (see the change block). The host-section exception is the point of the `hosts` design ([CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS]).
- verify-machine: [CXC-PC-NC-UNKNOWN-CORE-KEY] (a top-level `permisions` key is refused naming it); one table-driven test supplies each reserved HA name and asserts a refusal naming it; and the positive control (generic since r24): an unknown key inside `hosts.<foreign_host>` is ignored by this host and the plug-in installs, `foreign_host` being the conformance target's ([CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT] `foreign-host`; ComposeIO's value is "gnomon").
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- change: an earlier revision (r3) accepted and ignored thirty Home Assistant keys with a recorded warning, and refused `disabled`. r4 drops that: HA's names are reserved and refused like any unknown key.
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: r3 accepted-and-ignored the reserved and other documented HA integration-manifest keys with a warning, and refused `disabled` with its reason (the r3 `disabled` negative control).
  reasoning: v0.1 is not HA-manifest-compatible. A plain HA manifest is refused anyway for missing required fields (`contract`, `run`, `hosts`, `config_schema`) and the narrowed `version` and `requirements`, so tolerating its keys bought nothing a v0.1 user can reach, and cost two key lists, a warning channel and per-key tests. "Superset" is a later goal; HoP's own hedge: "(if indeed we use all of its interfaces in the first place)" (gnomon:GM-0216), and "Not critical for now - I mentioned HA integration for its design relevance, not to prioritize it on the schedule."
  rejected_alternative: accept-and-ignore HA keys with a recorded warning (r3).
  invalidating_assumption: if the HA bridge (gnomon GM-0215) or HoP's superset expectation is scheduled for a release, HA keys come back as tolerated or adopted fields in that MINOR.
  implication: the install record carries no warnings in v0.1; the r3 `disabled` negative control and its evidence slots are removed.
- provenance: architect-synthesis — architect rulings 2026-11-26 (audit C6/C7; reverses the earlier Q2 ruling).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-NOT-ADOPTED], [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS], [CXC-PC-NC-UNKNOWN-CORE-KEY]
- v0-2-versioned-keys: (0.2) `license` and `eula` are defined from contract 0.2. A manifest that states `contract: "0.1"` and carries either is REFUSED naming the key, like any key its stated contract does not define (CXC-PC-V02-VERSION-0-2). A 0.1 host refuses both keys as unknown, as today.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) `capabilities` and `warnings` are defined from contract 0.3. A manifest that states "0.1" or "0.2" and carries either is REFUSED naming the key, like any key its stated contract does not define (CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3).

### [CXC-PC-ADOPTED] The adopt record: what v0.1 takes from Home Assistant, Python entry points and WebExtensions, with sources

- ha-integration-manifest: adopted-from-HA — `domain`, `name`, `documentation`, `issue_tracker`, `codeowners` (HA's exact meaning and format: GitHub users or teams responsible, used by HA to route code review; made optional), `requirements` (narrowed to exact `==` pins), `documentation` (narrowed to https only), and `version` (narrowed to SemVer). Source https://contributors.home-assistant.io/core/integrations/manifest/ (read 2026-11-26; the page states no licence; home-assistant/core is Apache-2.0). What is adopted is field NAMES and MEANINGS, not code.
- ha-addon-config: adopted-from-HA-add-on — `description`. Source https://contributors.home-assistant.io/apps/reference/configuration/ (read 2026-11-26).
- narrowings: recorded as NARROWINGS of HA's rules, not as HA's own: (1) `version` SemVer only (HA: AwesomeVersion, SemVer or CalVer); (2) `requirements` exact `==` pins only (HA custom integrations allow any specifier); (3) `documentation` https only; and one RELAXATION: `codeowners` optional (HA requires it). Ruled by the architect at the maker-architect HA field-name check, 2026-11-26.
- contract-precedent: the HA add-on field `homeassistant` (the minimum Core version) is the precedent for our `contract` field: both pin the host version a package was written against. Ours is a contract version with the refusal rule of [CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE], not HA's minimum-version semantics.
- ha-breaking-versions: NOT adopted in v0.1 — the HA add-on `breaking_versions` (auto-update never crosses a listed version) moved to the reserved names with the whole `upgrade` block ([CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]); v0.1 sources are local only.
- entry-point-syntax: adopted — only the object-reference SYNTAX (`module:object.attr`), in `run.entry`. Source https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/entry-points/ (read 2026-11-26).
- webextensions-consent: adopted — the rule that an update which ADDS permissions prompts again ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING]). Source https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Mozilla/Add-ons/WebExtensions/manifest.json/permissions (and /optional_permissions, /host_permissions), read 2026-11-26. Deliberately NOT adopted: Firefox's behaviour in bug 1893232, which does not show host permissions added in an update.
- ha-config-flow-contrast: HA integrations have NO install-time permission or consent step (the manifest and integrations-index pages say nothing about one) and run in-process with full HA privileges; HA's config schemas are Python code objects (Probatio, a clean-room voluptuous reimplementation), not declarative JSON Schema. Consent and the declarative `config_schema` are therefore NEW here, not adopted.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — facts from the architect's prior-art research (plugin-manifest-prior-art.md, pages read 2026-11-26); the adoption choices are the architect's.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS], [CXC-PC-NOT-ADOPTED]

### [CXC-PC-NOT-ADOPTED] The not-adopted record: every item v0.1 leaves out, with its reason

- ha-runtime-and-catalogue-fields: NOT adopted — `iot_class`, `integration_type`, `categories`, `quality_scale`, `loggers`, `import_executor`, `preview_features`. Reason: HA-runtime or catalogue concepts that hello-world does not exercise. Names reserved so a later version adopts them with HA's meaning if it adopts them at all (see [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]; the two names the narrative's section 9 omitted were added at ratify).
- ha-addon-and-integration-fields-reserved: NOT adopted in v0.1, reserved at ratify — add-on `arch`, `stage`, `url`, and integration `disabled`. Reason: hello-world does not exercise them; see [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES].
- ha-fields-refused: every documented HA integration-manifest field that v0.1 does not adopt is RESERVED and REFUSED like any unknown key ([CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS], [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]); there is no accept-and-ignore list.
- ha-config-flow: NOT adopted — `config_flow`. Reason: our config is declarative ([CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]); HA's flows are Python code (now Probatio).
- ha-dependencies: NOT adopted — `dependencies`, `after_dependencies`. Reason: hello-world has none. Reserved with HA's meaning (other plug-in domains that must load first). Print-services may need it in v0.2.
- ha-discovery-matchers: NOT adopted — `mqtt`, `zeroconf`, `bluetooth`, `ssdp`, `homekit`, `lorawan`, `dhcp`, `usb`. Reason: a different meaning (device discovery). HA's `mqtt` is a discovery-topic list, NOT a bus declaration, which is why our block is `bus`.
- ha-addon-privilege-fields: NOT adopted — add-on `ports`, `map`, `devices`, `privileged`, and the API roles. Reason: hello-world needs no host privilege. The model is recorded for v0.2: these are the run-as-container analogue (see `run.form: process | container` at [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]).
- calver: NOT adopted — CalVer via AwesomeVersion. Reason: one scheme is simpler to compare in upgrade policy; v0.1 is SemVer only.
- silent-unknown-keys: NOT adopted — HA add-ons' silent ignore of unknown keys. Reason: [CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS].
- firefox-bug-1893232: NOT adopted — skipping the prompt for host permissions added in an update. Reason: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING].
- upgrade-block: NOT in v0.1 — the `upgrade` block (`auto`, `breaking_versions`). Reason: v0.1 package sources are a local directory or a wheel file only, so there is nothing to auto-upgrade from. Reserved ([CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]); the manual v1 to v2 upgrade stays in the lifecycle ([CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UPGRADE]).
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-ADOPTED], [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES], [CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS]
- entry-point-discovery: NOT adopted for discovery — Python entry points. Reason: [CXC-PC-DISCOVERY] `rule-no-entrypoint-discovery` (the r22 node CXC-PC-NO-ENTRYPOINT-DISCOVERY is in (a private draft, in the private companion), section CXC-PC-NO-ENTRYPOINT-DISCOVERY). Only the object-reference syntax is adopted.
- pluggy: NOT adopted. Reason: [CXC-PC-LIBRARY] `rule-not-built-on-pluggy` (the r22 node CXC-PC-NO-PLUGGY is in (a private draft, in the private companion), section CXC-PC-NO-PLUGGY).

### [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES] Names reserved for v0.2 and later, so v0.1 cannot collide with them

- rule: the following names are RESERVED. A manifest carrying any Home Assistant name listed in `ha-reserved-keys` or `ha-other-names-reserved` is REFUSED at install, naming the key, like any unknown key ([CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS]); the same holds for the other reserved items that are keys (`other-reserved`). A later MINOR release may adopt a reserved name, and MUST adopt it with the meaning recorded here.
- reserved-list: see `ha-reserved-keys` and `other-reserved`
- ha-reserved-keys: `dependencies`, `after_dependencies`, `iot_class`, `integration_type`, `categories`, `quality_scale`, `loggers`, `import_executor`, `preview_features`, `disabled` (HA integration manifest: a reason string that prevents loading), and the HA add-on fields `arch` (supported architectures), `stage` (`stable` | `experimental` | `deprecated`), `url` (project homepage, distinct from `documentation`), `image`, `ports`, `watchdog`, `map`, `devices`, `privileged`
- ha-other-names-reserved: the other documented HA integration-manifest names, reserved with HA's meaning and refused in v0.1: `config_flow`, `single_config_entry`, `mqtt`, `zeroconf`, `ssdp`, `homekit`, `bluetooth`, `dhcp`, `usb`, `lorawan`, `supported_by`, `iot_standards`.
- expected-v0-2: print-services (the proving step after hello-world) is expected to need `dependencies` or none, plus network-outbound access (IPP to a printer's IP), which since r21 it can DECLARE in v0.1 through `permissions` ([CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS]); the bus-participant extension (third step, [CXC-PC-PROVING-ORDER]) needs the selector-scoped bus permission form, so v0.2 is likely sized by them.
- verify-machine: one table-driven test supplies each key in `ha-reserved-keys` and `ha-other-names-reserved` and asserts a refusal naming it; the items in `other-reserved` that are KEY names or values (`upgrade`, `run.form: process`, `run.form: container`) are refused naming them; items that are not key names (the selector-scoped permission form, git URLs and indexes) are checked where they arise ([CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL] refuses a git URL source) and are not tested as keys here. (r21) `permissions` is no longer reserved; it is tested at [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS].
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-NOT-ADOPTED], [CXC-PC-RULE-SIZE], [CXC-PC-PRE-INTEGRATION], [CXC-PC-PROVING-ORDER]

