## 5. Derive once, consent before any code runs

### [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE] The static manifest is authoritative for consent, the class for runtime; drift is caught at the one moment both are visible

- claim: spec 85 says the author writes ONE declaration and everything is derived from it; consent must render before any plug-in code is imported. Resolution: (1) the author writes the `ComposePlugin` class plus a hand-written CORE manifest (identity, run, hosts); (2) a build command (`compose-plugin build`) generates the `config_schema` and `bus` blocks INTO the shipped `manifest.json` from the class; (3) at install, AFTER consent and BEFORE enable, the host imports the class and checks that its derived declaration still matches the manifest. A mismatch REFUSES the enable, with the diff named.
- rule: the host MUST NOT import the plug-in's module before consent is granted. The class is authoritative for runtime; the static manifest is authoritative for consent.
- config-schema-always-present: `config_schema` is ALWAYS present in a BUILT manifest. A plug-in without a `Config` model gets the schema of an EMPTY OBJECT. A manifest lacking `config_schema` is REFUSED at install as "not built". Ruled architect-synthesis, ruled 2026-11-26 at ratify (pre-integration q6).
- default: when the class declares no publishes or subscribes, the build omits the `bus` block, and the drift check treats the absent block as the empty declaration.
- boundary-case: a shipped manifest whose `config_schema` differs from the schema derived from the class, or whose `bus` differs from the class's publishes/subscribes, is class/manifest DRIFT: the enable is refused, the diff names each differing path, and nothing is half-enabled.
- verify-machine: [CXC-PC-NC-CLASS-MANIFEST-DRIFT]; a plug-in with no `Config` builds a manifest whose `config_schema` is the empty-object schema; a manifest with `config_schema` removed is refused at install with the text "not built"; and an import spy shows no import of the plug-in module between manifest read and consent grant.
- verify-human: the refusal text on the glass names the drifting field and shows both values, readable by an operator who has not seen the code.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — resolves the tension between spec 85's derive-once and consent-before-code.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FILE], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT], [CXC-PC-NC-CLASS-MANIFEST-DRIFT]
- bus-types-attribute: A ComposePlugin that declares bus traffic MUST define the class attribute `bus_types: ClassVar[dict[str, Literal["STATE", "EVENT"]]]`, mapping each namespace to its type, and covering EXACTLY the namespaces in `publishes` and `subscribes`. `compose-plugin build` emits `{namespace, type}` entries from those lists plus `bus_types`. The build fails loudly if a namespace has no type, or if `bus_types` names a namespace in neither list. The enable-time drift check, run on EVERY enable, compares the class's derived pairs with the manifest's pairs, so both namespace drift and type drift are refused. composebus and plugin.py are unchanged; the host merely looks for the attribute. Limit: one namespace has one type. Reason: spec 85 declares publishes and subscribes as bare namespace strings, so the class could not otherwise supply the type the manifest requires. Provenance: architect-synthesis, composeux-architect 2026-11-26 (r16), on the second-wave builder's question; binds both hosts.

---

## 6. Layer H: consent

### [CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT] The library renders the consent CONTENT from the manifest alone; the host decides the GATE

- claim: the library renders consent content; the host decides the gate ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES]).
- rule: the consent content MUST come from the manifest alone and MUST include: name, version, description, codeowners (shown as "code owners", NOT as a verified publisher identity), documentation, run form and isolation (the ACTUAL level the host will use, shown as "runs as: <level>"), `requirements` (the code it installs), every bus publish and every bus subscribe, every declared permission with its purpose (r21, [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS], `permission-lines`), and each host's own host-section permissions (the host supplies a renderer for its section). The content sits under the dialog title of `dialog-title` (r21).
- boundary-case: a field absent from the manifest (for example `codeowners`) is shown as absent, never omitted silently.
- verify-machine: rendering the consent content for hello-world v1 yields exactly the expected list including 1 publish STATE, 1 publish EVENT, 1 subscribe EVENT, no permissions heading and no permission line, and the title "<name> <version>  Manifest: Declared by its Developer" built from hello-world v1's manifest `name` and `version` fields (this spec does not fix hello-world's display name; the 45bef44 fixture's manifest gives "Hello World" and "1.0.0"); a manifest declaring one network and one filesystem entry yields the heading "Declared by the plug-in; not checked" exactly once, followed by exactly two permission lines in the `permission-lines` format, none of which carries a per-line mark; the render makes no network call and no import of the plug-in module.
- verify-human: an operator reads the hello-world consent screen and can state, from the screen alone, what code it installs and what it may publish and subscribe to.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES], [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS], [CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]
- license-line: (0.2) When `license` is declared, the content carries ONE line `license: <expression> (declared by the plug-in; not verified)`, placed after the `documentation` line and before the run form. When it is not declared there is NO licence line. This is a deliberate exception to the boundary-case 'a field absent from the manifest is shown as absent', so that the consent content of a manifest with no licence is byte-identical to 0.1's apart from the page footer's contract version.
- eula-block: (0.2) When `eula` is declared, the content carries the EULA block of CXC-PC-V02-EULA-DISPLAY after the last permission line, in the web screen and in `ConsentContent.text()`. When it is not declared there is no block.
- package-file-exception: (0.2) The rule 'the consent content MUST come from the manifest alone' is read with one addition: the EULA file that the manifest's `eula` names is read by the package reader and is part of the content. It is read like `standard_questions` is checked, without importing or executing any plug-in code and without a network call; the existing `verify-machine` assertion of no network call and no import holds for it.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) When a manifest that states "0.3" declares `capabilities`, the content carries one `capabilities:` line after the permission lines (CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES); when it or the host declares warnings, the content carries a warnings block after the EULA block, each warning with its own acknowledgement (CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS, CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK). A grant-bearing config property adds one advisory line (CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG). A manifest with none of these renders as under 0.2.

### [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD] A consent record is kept for audit, even after uninstall

- granted-format: `granted` is a sorted, de-duplicated list of exactly five string forms: `bus.publish:<namespace>:<TYPE>`, `bus.subscribe:<namespace>:<TYPE>`, `code:<pep503-name>==<version>` (per [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING] `code-is-a-permission`), `permission.network:<direction>:<target>` and `permission.filesystem:<access>:<path>` (r21, one per declared entry of [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS]; the purpose text is not part of the string), plus host-section strings prefixed `<host>:`, whose vocabulary the host spec defines. Every consent-record text field (decided_by, host, domain, version and any other string) obeys BOTH the blank rule and the one-line rule ([CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS] `validator-readings` (12), N3), so no record can carry a forged second line. A change of `isolation_floor`: RAISING it never widens, because more isolation weakens nothing. LOWERING it WIDENS, and requires fresh consent ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING]), whenever the host would actually run the plug-in at a lower isolation level than before, because the "runs as" level shown at consent would drop. A host that always runs at subprocess (for example Gnomon) is unaffected, because its "runs as" level cannot drop.
- claim: every consent decision produces a record `{domain, version, manifest_sha256, granted: [...], decided_by, decided_at, host, decision}` where `decision` is `granted` or `refused`, and `decided_at` is taken from the clock. (0.2) A record made for a manifest that declares `eula` also carries the optional key `eula_sha256`; see `optional-keys`.
- rule: the record MUST be kept after the plug-in is uninstalled. A refusal is recorded as well as a grant.
- verify-machine: after step 9 of [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT] the record for hello-world v1 and v2 is still present in the store, and the refused-widening control ([CXC-PC-NC-WIDENING-REFUSED]) leaves a record with `decision: refused`.
- verify-human: the Plugin Manager's consent-records panel in each host shows the record after uninstall.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UNINSTALL], [CXC-PC-EVIDENCE]
- optional-keys: (0.2) The record has the eight keys above and ONE optional key, `eula_sha256` (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HASH-RECORD): omitted when no EULA is declared, never null. The reader accepts the eight keys, plus that key when it is 64 lowercase hex, and refuses any other key set naming the key. `granted-format` is unchanged: the EULA hash is not a grant.
- record-decline: (0.2) A `refused` record may also be written by `PluginManager.record_decline` (CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE), through the same append. The rule 'a refusal is recorded as well as a grant' is unchanged and now has a path that does not run an install or an upgrade.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) The record may carry a second optional key, `acknowledged_warnings` (CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK); `granted-format` is unchanged because a warning is not a grant. The reader accepts the eight keys plus `eula_sha256` and `acknowledged_warnings`, each only when valid (CXC-PC-V03-STORES-COMPAT).

### [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING] An upgrade that WIDENS the granted set requires fresh consent

- widening-definition: an upgrade WIDENS iff the new `granted` set (format at [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD] `granted-format`) is NOT a subset of the old one, OR it lowers `isolation_floor` so that the host would actually run the plug-in at a lower isolation level than before, because the "runs as" level would drop. RAISING `isolation_floor` never widens. A host that always runs at subprocess (for example Gnomon) is unaffected by the floor clause. In the subset test a granted `permission.filesystem:read-write:<path>` covers `permission.filesystem:read:<path>` (r21, `permissions-widen`).
- rule: an upgrade that WIDENS (`widening-definition`: its manifest adds anything to the granted set, or it lowers `isolation_floor` so that the host's actual "runs as" level would drop) MUST require fresh consent before it is applied. Only an upgrade that does not widen (the same or a narrower set, and no drop in the actual "runs as" level) follows the host's policy.
- why: this follows WebExtensions, and deliberately NOT Firefox's bug 1893232, which skipped prompts for host permissions added in an update.
- boundary-case: refused widening consent leaves the installed version, its config, its enabled/disabled state and its consent record decision `refused` for the attempt; no part of the new version is activated.
- verify-machine: hello-world v1 to v2 adds `bus.subscribe:plugins/hello_world/name:EVENT`, so the new set is not a subset of the old and the upgrade MUST present a consent screen showing exactly `plugins/hello_world/name` EVENT as added ([CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT] step 6); [CXC-PC-NC-WIDENING-REFUSED] covers refusal. (r21) Permissions cases, run as library tests outside the 9-step script: a v1 declaring `outbound printer.local:631` upgraded to a v2 that adds `outbound printer.local:9100` presents re-consent showing exactly `permission.network:outbound:printer.local:9100` as added; a v2 that only rewords that entry's `purpose` presents no re-consent; a v1 declaring `read /tmp/hello` upgraded to `read-write /tmp/hello` presents re-consent; a v1 declaring `read-write /tmp/hello` upgraded to `read /tmp/hello` presents no re-consent; a v2 that removes an entry presents no re-consent.
- verify-human: the operator sees the ADDED permission highlighted on the re-consent screen.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — follows MDN's WebExtensions permissions pages (read 2026-11-26).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-ADOPTED], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UPGRADE], [CXC-PC-NC-WIDENING-REFUSED]
- permissions-widen: (r21) Declared permissions widen under the existing subset rule (`widening-definition`), through their grant strings at [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD] `granted-format`, with one covering reading: for the subset test, a granted `permission.filesystem:read-write:<path>` covers `permission.filesystem:read:<path>`. So an upgrade RE-ASKS consent only when it adds a permission entry, names a new `target`, `path` or `direction`, or changes `access` from `read` to `read-write`. A NARROWING is not a widening and does not re-ask: changing `read-write` to `read`, or removing an entry. A changed `purpose` alone is not a widening either, because the purpose is not part of the grant string; the re-consent screen, when one is shown for another reason, shows the new purpose.
- eula-reconsent: (0.2) A second, independent trigger for fresh consent on upgrade: the new package declares a `eula` whose digest differs from the installed record's `eula_sha256` (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT). `widening-definition`, `permissions-widen` and `code-is-a-permission` are unchanged. Where `rule` says an upgrade 'that does not widen follows the host's policy', a changed EULA is read as requiring the gate, whatever the grant sets say.

### [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES] The gates: Gnomon waits for HoP; ComposeIO needs an operator click or an explicit `--consent`

- boundary-case: a CLI install without `--consent` and without an interactive operator refuses; a plug-in listed in a config file is NOT thereby consented.
- verify-machine: on ComposeIO a config-file entry naming an uninstalled plug-in does not install it; a CLI install without `--consent` exits non-zero and writes no install record; with `--consent` the consent record's `decided_by` is non-empty.
- verify-human: HoP confirms on Gnomon that install, widening upgrade and uninstall each wait.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: david-ruling for the Gnomon half (D1); architect-synthesis for the ComposeIO half (proposed, to be ratified).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-RULE-CONSENT-HOST-OWNED], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD]
- v0-2-record-decline: (0.2) A host can record an operator's decline without running install or upgrade, with `PluginManager.record_decline` (CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE). Each host's gate is unchanged.
- v0-2-eula-must-show: (0.2) A host that offers consent for a manifest that declares `eula` MUST show the EULA text, or refuse the install naming `eula` (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HOST-OBLIGATION). The gates themselves are unchanged.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) The gate runs exactly at install and at an upgrade that widens grants, changes the EULA, or adds or changes a capability token or a warning; enable, disable, configure and uninstall take no gate and need the host's authenticated operator (CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS). An operator's refuse or cancel stops any operation, gate or not (CXC-PC-V03-REFUSE-ALWAYS-STOPS).

---

## 7. Lifecycle

### [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-STATES] The lifecycle states and transitions

- claim: `available`, then (consent) `installed`, then `enabled` and `disabled` (toggling both ways), then `uninstalled`. Upgrade runs from `installed` or `enabled`. Spec 85's `parked(reason)` is a SUB-STATE of enabled.
- rule: every transition not listed MUST be refused naming the current state and the requested operation.
- verify-machine: a table-driven test attempts every operation from every state and asserts that only the listed transitions succeed; for example `enable` from `available` is refused.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UPGRADE], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-DISABLE], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UNINSTALL]
- v0-2-install-log: (0.2) Every transition in this list is one event of installs.jsonl, written before the snapshot (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-EVENT, CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-WRITE-ORDER). The set of allowed transitions is unchanged.

### [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL] Install: fetch, verify manifest_sha256 against the consented manifest, install into a host-managed location, record consent

- rule: install MUST take the package from a local directory or a wheel file, verify `manifest_sha256` against the CONSENTED manifest, install into a host-managed location, and record consent. The sha256 is taken over the manifest that was shown at consent.
- manifest-sha256-definition: `manifest_sha256` is the SHA-256 of the EXACT BYTES of the shipped `manifest.json` file, after `compose-plugin build` generated its blocks ([CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]), with NO canonicalization (no whitespace or key-order normalization).
- package-sources: v0.1 package sources are a LOCAL DIRECTORY or a WHEEL FILE only. Git URLs and package indexes are reserved for v0.2 ([CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]); a git URL or index reference is refused naming the source kind.
- manifest-read-location: the host reads `manifest.json` from the directory root, or from inside the wheel zip as package data at `<import-package>/manifest.json`, WITHOUT executing any build backend or plug-in code.
- boundary-case: sha mismatch between the manifest shown at consent and the manifest in the fetched package refuses the install ([CXC-PC-NC-TAMPERED-MANIFEST]); a refused install leaves no files in the install root.
- verify-machine: conformance step 1 installs v1 and the control [CXC-PC-NC-TAMPERED-MANIFEST] refuses a tampered manifest; the consented digest equals `sha256sum manifest.json` of the shipped file, and a re-serialization of the same JSON with different whitespace yields a DIFFERENT digest and is refused; a wheel install reads `<import-package>/manifest.json` with an import spy and a build-backend spy both recording zero calls; a git URL source is refused.
- verify-human: none required beyond the consent screen.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-CONTENT], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-STATES], [CXC-PC-PRE-INTEGRATION], [CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]
- requirements-in-v0-1: A v0.1 host REFUSES at install any manifest whose `requirements` is not `[]`, naming each pin, with a message that states the reason: "requirements are not installed in contract v0.1: installing a pin also installs its transitive dependencies, which are code you never consented to, and needs a package index; installing requirements arrives in v0.2". The `code:` grant form and its widening rule stay defined for v0.2; they cannot be reached in v0.1 and are tested at library level. hello-world declares `requirements: []`.
- v0-2-eula-and-log: (0.2) `install` gains the optional argument `consented_eula_sha256`, required when the manifest declares `eula` (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HASH-RECORD), and appends its `install` event after the gate and before the code is placed. The rule, the sha rule and the refusals above are unchanged.

### [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-CONFIG] Config is validated against `config_schema` BEFORE instantiation; invalid config parks loudly

- rule: config MUST be validated against `config_schema` before the plug-in is instantiated; invalid config PARKS the plug-in loudly with `config:` reasons. This REUSES spec 87's [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION] and error taxonomy; it does not respecify them.
- verify-machine: [CXC-PC-NC-INVALID-CONFIG]; conformance step 2 sets `greeting` to a valid string and step 3 starts.
- verify-human: the parked plug-in's reason is readable on the Plugin Manager and names the offending field.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION], [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY], [CXC-PC-NC-INVALID-CONFIG]
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) For a manifest that states "0.3" a grant-bearing `config_schema` property draws a named advisory, a structural author aid and not enforcement (CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG); `configure` takes no gate and writes one lifecycle entry that never carries the value (CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD).

### [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-HEALTH] Health comes from the runtime contract; v0.1 has no `health` manifest field

- claim: the host's loader publishes status (spec 85 [CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE]; spec 87 [CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE]). Health states are `running`, `parked(<reason>)`, `degraded(<reason>)` (reported by the plug-in), `stopped` and `failed(<reason>)`.
- rule: a v0.1 manifest MUST NOT carry a `health` field, because health comes from the runtime contract ([CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS] refuses it).
- verify-machine: hello-world with `greeting == ""` reports `degraded("empty greeting")` through the status surface; a manifest carrying `health` is refused.
- verify-human: the Plugin Manager shows the five states distinguishably.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE], [CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1]
- degraded-reporting: A plug-in MAY define a method `health_reason(self) -> str | None` on its ComposePlugin class. The host calls it after start and on each status tick. A non-None return means the state is `degraded(<return>)`, and the plug-in keeps running. In the subprocess runner, the child converts it into a `_ctl: degraded` frame. This adds nothing to composebus: `plugin.py` is unchanged, and the host merely looks for the method. A retained health topic was rejected, because it would put a permission on every plug-in just so it can report being unwell. Provenance: architect-synthesis, composeux-architect 2026-11-26 (r15), on the second-wave builder's question Q1; binds both hosts.

### [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UPGRADE] Upgrade is manual in v0.1: stored config is kept and re-validated, and the host never auto-upgrades

- boundary-case: an upgrade that widens permissions requires fresh consent first ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING]); a manifest carrying an `upgrade` block is refused as unknown in v0.1 ([CXC-PC-RESERVED-NAMES]).
- verify-machine: conformance step 6 (stored `greeting` kept, `repeat` takes its default 1); a stored config invalid under a v2 variant parks with a `config:` reason and is not rewritten; a host started with a newer wheel in its source directory does NOT upgrade by itself.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-CONFIG], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V2]
- v0-2-eula-reconsent: (0.2) An upgrade also requires fresh consent when its EULA digest differs from the installed one (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT). Stored-config handling, manual-only upgrade and the reserved `upgrade` block are unchanged.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) An upgrade with no gate and `accepted=False` aborts with no change and writes a `decline` entry (CXC-PC-V03-REFUSE-ALWAYS-STOPS); the 0.2.0 behaviour, which upgrades whatever the operator chose, is the registered defect named there. Stored-config handling and manual-only upgrade are unchanged.

### [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-DISABLE] Disable stops the plug-in and keeps install, config and consent

- rule: disable MUST stop the plug-in and keep its installed code, its stored config and its consent record. Re-enable MUST restore it to `running` with the same config, without fresh consent.
- verify-machine: conformance steps 7 and 8; the config value from step 2 is unchanged after step 8.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-STATES], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD]
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) Disable and re-enable take no consent gate; each is one lifecycle entry carrying the operator (CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS, CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD).

### [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UNINSTALL] Uninstall stops, tombstones retained topics, removes code and config, keeps the consent record

- rule: uninstall MUST stop the plug-in, TOMBSTONE the plug-in's retained topics (the print_services pattern), remove its code and its config, and KEEP the consent record.
- verify-machine: conformance step 9: the retained STATE topic `plugins/hello_world/greeting` is tombstoned on the bus, no code remains in the install root, no config remains, and the consent records for v1 and v2 remain.
- verify-human: an operator reading the bus after uninstall sees the tombstone, not a stale greeting.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-STATES]
- v0-2-web-confirm: (0.2) The library's web manager puts a confirm step in front of this operation (CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM). `PluginManager.uninstall` itself is unchanged, and the operation appends its `uninstall` event before it stops the plug-in.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) The uninstall confirm offers keep (default) or delete for the plug-in's data folder, and the choice is written to the uninstall entry (CXC-PC-V03-UNINSTALL-DATA-CHOICE). The rule above is unchanged.

---

## 8. Discovery and the registry (settles DD-CSL-DISCOVERY and DD-CSL-MANIFEST-FORMAT)

### [CXC-PC-DISCOVERY] The host's install records ARE the registry; neither scanning entry points nor importing installed packages is discovery

- claim: discovery is the host's INSTALL RECORDS. A plug-in is known to a host when a consented install record exists; the host never learns of a plug-in by scanning entry points or by importing installed packages.
- settles: [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY]
- rule: spec 87's tenant manifest `{plugin, config}` ([CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE]) now names an INSTALLED plug-in by its `domain`. The dotted-import-path form stays for BUILT-IN default plug-ins only ([CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGINS]).
- boundary-case: a tenant manifest entry naming a `domain` with no install record parks with a `resolve:` reason naming the domain ([CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY]); it never falls back to importing a package of that name.
- verify-machine: an installed distribution that exposes an entry point in the plug-in group is NOT loaded unless an install record exists for its `domain`; a tenant manifest naming a `domain` without an install record parks with `resolve:`.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [DD-CSL-DISCOVERY], [CXC-CSL-MANIFEST-SHAPE]
- rule-no-entrypoint-discovery: a host MUST NOT discover plug-ins by enumerating Python entry points.

---

## 9. The shared management library and the plain web UI

### [CXC-PC-LIBRARY] The library is a standalone distribution so no plug-in host needs a ComposeIO install

- claim: the shared management library is a STANDALONE distribution named `compose-plugins`, import name `compose_plugins`. It depends on `composebus` (for `ComposePlugin` and schema export) plus the standard library, lives in ComposeIO's public repo at `python/compose_plugins/`, and is released by tag like composebus.
- rule: it MUST NOT depend on any ComposeIO distribution ([CXC-PC-RULE-BUS-OPTIONAL]).
- verify-machine: the built wheel's metadata `Requires-Dist` is exactly `composebus` (no ComposeIO distribution, no `pluggy`); `pip install compose-plugins` into a clean venv with no ComposeIO installed succeeds and `import compose_plugins` works. The wheel listing is recorded at [CXC-PC-EVIDENCE].
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: david-ruling for the contract living in ComposeIO's public repo (gnomon:GM-0216); architect-synthesis for the standalone distribution, its name and its dependency list; full provenance in the r23 revision record
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIBRARY-CONTENTS], [CXC-PC-RULE-BUS-OPTIONAL], [CXC-PC-RELEASE-TAGS]
- rule-not-built-on-pluggy: the contract MUST NOT be built on pluggy hook specs.

### [CXC-PC-LIBRARY-CONTENTS] What the library holds

- claim: the library holds: the manifest JSON Schema and validator; the registry and install records; the install, upgrade, enable, disable and uninstall operations; the consent-record store and the consent-content renderer; a `Host` protocol each host implements (the consent gate, the install root, run/stop through the host's runtime, the status source, the auth hook of [CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS], and the `extra_panels` hook); the subprocess runner ([CXC-PC-SUBPROCESS-RUNNER]); and the plain web Plugin Manager UI ([CXC-PC-WEB-UI]). The `compose-plugin build` command ([CXC-PC-DERIVE-ONCE]) ships with it.
- extra_panels: the `Host` protocol member through which the host supplies EXTRA Plugin Manager panels, rendered under the same URL prefix and the same auth ([CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS]); this is how Gnomon extends the manager. Architect ruling 2026-11-26 (audit Q3).
- verify-machine: the built wheel lists a module for each item; a stub `Host` implementing only the protocol members drives the full conformance script against a fake runtime.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIBRARY], [CXC-PC-WEB-UI], [CXC-PC-SUBPROCESS-RUNNER]
- v0-2-additions: (0.2) The library also holds: the install event log `InstallLog` (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-FILE) and its divergence check (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-DIVERGENCE); the public call `PluginManager.record_decline` (CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE); the licence validator and the EULA reader (CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD, CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD). The `Host` protocol gains no member (CXC-PC-V02-STORES-COMPAT).
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) The library also holds: the optional `Host.policy` and `Host.data_dir` members (CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK, CXC-PC-V03-UNINSTALL-DATA-CHOICE); the third schema file `manifest-0.3.schema.json` (CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3); the grant-free config advisory (CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG). The 0.2 additions and the rule that a `Host` written against an earlier contract runs unchanged still hold (CXC-PC-V03-STORES-COMPAT).

### [CXC-PC-WEB-UI] The Plugin Manager UI is plain: server-rendered HTML, minimal JS, a mountable WSGI app

- claim: the UI is server-rendered HTML with minimal JS, served as a mountable WSGI app. Gnomon embeds it in its Configuration tab and adds its own panels (agents, messages) through the `extra_panels` hook of `Host` ([CXC-PC-LIBRARY-CONTENTS]); the consent-records panel is part of the shared manager ([CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD]), not a Gnomon addition. ComposeIO serves it from ComposeServer.
- verify-machine: the app is a WSGI callable; a WSGI test client lists installed plug-ins, performs install, enable, disable and uninstall through its forms, and every response is HTML with no required JS to complete a lifecycle operation.
- verify-human: an operator completes the hello-world lifecycle in a browser on each host.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling — HoP, gnomon:GM-0216 (a plain web UI, extendable for Gnomon); the WSGI shape and the extra_panels hook are architect-synthesis; full provenance in the r23 revision record
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-UI-URL-PREFIX], [CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS], [CXC-PC-COMPOSEIO-HOST-IDENTITY]
- v0-2-uninstall-confirm: (0.2) The route set gains `POST .../plugin/<domain>/uninstall/prepare` and `POST .../plugin/<domain>/uninstall/decide`, and the route `POST .../plugin/<domain>/uninstall` no longer uninstalls (CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM). The consent screen shows a declared EULA (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-DISPLAY). The index page lists install-log findings when there are any.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) The no-gate upgrade screen is labelled "Upgrade" and "Cancel" (CXC-PC-V03-REFUSE-ALWAYS-STOPS); the consent screen carries a control per warning (CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK); the uninstall confirm carries the data choice (CXC-PC-V03-UNINSTALL-DATA-CHOICE).

### [CXC-PC-UI-URL-PREFIX] The mounted app MUST work under a URL prefix: SCRIPT_NAME-correct links, no absolute paths

- rule: the WSGI app MUST generate every link, form action and redirect relative to `SCRIPT_NAME`; it MUST contain NO absolute path that ignores the mount prefix.
- default: an empty `SCRIPT_NAME` (mounted at the root) works.
- verify-machine: mount the app under `/a/plugins`, `/gnomon/config/plugins` and the empty prefix; crawl every reachable page; every `href`, `src`, `action` and `Location` header either is relative or begins with the active `SCRIPT_NAME`; ZERO begin with `/` outside that prefix.
- verify-human: HoP opens the manager inside Gnomon's Configuration tab and follows every link without leaving the tab's prefix.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the ask is gnomon-architect's UI requirement.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-WEB-UI], [CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS]
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) The rule is unchanged and is now also a tested requirement for the mount `/plugin-manager`, covering every 0.3 screen (CXC-PC-V03-MOUNTABLE-WEB-MANAGER).

### [CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS] The UI takes BOTH its auth hook and its consent gate from the host's `Host` implementation; the library does no authentication

- rule: the library MUST NOT authenticate. Every request MUST be passed through the host's auth hook, supplied by the host's `Host` implementation, before any handler runs; every state-changing operation that needs consent MUST obtain it through the host's consent gate, also from `Host`.
- extra_panels: the host MAY supply extra panels through `Host.extra_panels`; each is rendered under the SAME URL prefix ([CXC-PC-UI-URL-PREFIX]) and behind the SAME auth hook as the shared pages. Architect ruling 2026-11-26 (audit Q3).
- boundary-case: with an auth hook that denies, every route yields a non-2xx response and NO handler body executes; with no auth hook supplied the app refuses to start (it never defaults to open).
- verify-machine: a `Host` supplying one extra panel shows it at a `SCRIPT_NAME`-prefixed URL, and the denying auth hook blocks it like every other route; a `Host` whose auth hook denies: crawl every route, assert non-2xx and that a handler-side-effect canary never fires; constructing the app with a `Host` lacking an auth hook raises at construction.
- verify-human: on Gnomon the manager shows only after HoP's session is authenticated by Gnomon's own mechanism.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the ask is gnomon-architect's.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-WEB-UI], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES], [CXC-PC-LIBRARY-CONTENTS]
- csrf-defence: The shared Plugin Manager MUST refuse every state-changing request (each POST: install/consent, configure, enable, disable, upgrade decide, uninstall) unless it carries a per-session CSRF token (see `csrf-token-scope` for what "per-session" means) that matches the one issued in the rendered form. It MUST also refuse any state-changing request whose `Origin` is present and is not the host's own origin, or whose `Sec-Fetch-Site` is `cross-site`. A refused request changes nothing and records nothing; it never becomes a consent record. Reason: a browser re-sends cached HTTP Basic credentials on a cross-site form POST to the loopback origin, so without this, a page the operator merely visits could grant consent under the operator's name. Provenance: architect-synthesis, composeux-architect 2026-11-26 (r18), on independent QA FAIL 1; a library rule, so it binds both hosts.
- csrf-token-scope: "Per-session" for the csrf-defence token means per operator, per app instance: an HMAC of the operator name under a key created when the app starts, which rotates at every restart. Basic authentication has no session to bind to, and a cross-site page cannot read the token under the same-origin policy.
- v0-2-new-routes: (0.2) The two new uninstall routes are POSTs: every request passes the host's auth hook first and the `csrf-defence` below, exactly as the other state-changing routes do. `csrf-defence` and `csrf-token-scope` are unchanged.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) A host that mounts the web manager also keeps every state-changing route out of agents' reach, a host obligation the library cannot check (CXC-PC-V03-HOST-AGENT-BLOCK).

