## 10. The subprocess runner

### [CXC-PC-SUBPROCESS-RUNNER] The subprocess runner exists, lives in `compose_plugins`, and is built once for both hosts

- claim: spec 87 [CXC-CSL-SUBPROCESS-PENDING] only RECORDS a subprocess-floor plug-in; it does not run it. v0.1 requires the runner to exist. It lives in `compose_plugins`, built once and available to every host. It stays in v0.1 because a host that chooses the subprocess level needs it ([CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE]). It realizes spec 84 [CXC-CBK-SUBPROCESS-DAUGHTERCARD]: a child process holds the plug-in, and its bus traffic reaches the host's ComposeBus over a local IPC connector.
- ipc-framing: NEWLINE-DELIMITED JSON over the inherited socketpair. BUS frames use composebus's `BusMessage` JSON wire form (`BusMessage.to_json` / `from_json`). CONTROL frames are JSON objects with a top-level `_ctl` key whose value is one of `configure`, `start`, `stop`, `health`, `degraded`. Ruled architect-synthesis, ruled 2026-11-26 at ratify (pre-integration q7).
- rule: (1) when a host runs the plug-in at the subprocess level, the plug-in runs in a CHILD process, never in the host process; (2) a child crash MUST be reported as `failed(<reason>)`; (3) the host's loop MUST NOT die with the child; (4) the child's bus link is local-only by construction ([CXC-PC-RUNNER-LOCAL-ONLY]).
- change: spec 87 [CXC-CSL-SUBPROCESS-PENDING] records a subprocess-floor plug-in as `subprocess_pending` and never starts it. With this runner a host that has the runner MUST run a subprocess-level plug-in through it instead of leaving it pending.
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: spec 87 holds subprocess-floor plug-ins in `subprocess_pending` because the Phase-1 daughtercard host did not exist.
  reasoning: a host that chooses the subprocess level (gnomon-architect's host does, per Gnomon spec 50) cannot run a plug-in without a runner, so v0.1 cannot ship without it; building it once in the shared library avoids two implementations.
  rejected_alternative: each host builds its own subprocess runner.
  invalidating_assumption: if spec 84's daughtercard host is delivered separately by the kernel, this runner becomes a thin adapter over it rather than a second implementation.
  implication: spec 87's pending seam remains for hosts that do not carry the runner; [CXC-CSL-SUBPROCESS-PENDING] is not edited by this spec.
- boundary-case: a child that exits non-zero, is killed by a signal, or never completes its start handshake is `failed(<reason>)` with the exit code or signal named; the host keeps serving other plug-ins.
- verify-machine: every line the runner writes or reads over the socketpair parses as one JSON object; a bus frame round-trips through `BusMessage.to_json` / `from_json`; a control frame with an unlisted `_ctl` value is refused loudly; a fixture plug-in that raises in `start` and one that calls `os._exit(1)` each yield `failed(<reason>)` while a sibling plug-in keeps responding on the bus; the host process id and its event loop are unchanged.
- verify-human: an operator kills the hello-world child (`kill -9`) and sees `failed(<reason>)` in the Plugin Manager while the host stays up.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-SUBPROCESS-DAUGHTERCARD], [CXC-CSL-SUBPROCESS-PENDING], [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR], [CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE], [CXC-PC-PRE-INTEGRATION]

### [CXC-PC-RUNNER-LOCAL-ONLY] The child's bus link is local-only BY CONSTRUCTION: an AF_UNIX socketpair created by the host and inherited by the child

- rule: the link between the host and the child MUST be an `AF_UNIX` `socketpair()` created by the HOST and INHERITED by the child, so there is no listener and no filesystem path. A TCP port, or any other network-reachable listener, is FORBIDDEN.
- platforms: macOS and Linux (WSL counts as Linux). On a platform where `socket.socketpair()` does not yield `AF_UNIX`, the runner REFUSES TO START (fail loud) and NEVER falls back to TCP. Ruled architect-synthesis, ruled 2026-11-26 at ratify (pre-integration q7).
- why: a prohibition you cannot express is stronger than one you must remember to check ([CXC-CS-ISOLATION-ENCODING]'s grain): with an inherited socket nothing is listening, so there is nothing for another process or host to connect to.
- verify-machine: during a conformance run at the subprocess level, the child process holds NO inet socket (checked with `lsof` or `psutil`: zero `AF_INET`/`AF_INET6` file descriptors), AND the host opened NO listening socket for it (no `LISTEN` socket in the host's descriptors attributable to the runner); with `socket.socketpair` patched to return an `AF_INET` pair, the runner refuses to start and opens no TCP socket.
- verify-human: none required — fully machine-observable.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — normative, at gnomon-architect's request, 2026-11-26.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-SUBPROCESS-RUNNER], [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-ENCODING], [CXC-PC-PRE-INTEGRATION]

### [CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE] Each host chooses its isolation level, at least the plug-in's declared floor; the contract assumes neither level (non-normative pointer)

- rule: a host picks ANY isolation level >= the plug-in's declared floor (spec 85, [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]) and records its choice in its OWN host spec. The contract MUST NOT assume in-process or subprocess.
- example-only: Gnomon's choice to run every Python `ComposePlugin` as a SUBPROCESS only (so a plug-in crash cannot take its hub down) is recorded in Gnomon spec 50, NOT here. This node does not make it normative.
- consequence: the subprocess runner stays in v0.1 ([CXC-PC-SUBPROCESS-RUNNER]) because a host that chooses subprocess needs it; the conformance script runs hello-world at EVERY level the host supports ([CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT]).
- raised-floor: the host raises a lower floor SILENTLY (no notice event, since higher isolation weakens nothing), and the ACTUAL level is always shown: on the consent screen ("runs as: subprocess") and in the plug-in's status. The opposite case is an upgrade that LOWERS `isolation_floor`: on a host that runs a plug-in at its declared floor, if the host would actually run it at a lower level than before, the upgrade WIDENS and requires fresh consent, because the "runs as" level would drop; raising the floor never widens; a host that always runs at subprocess (for example Gnomon) is unaffected. Rule text at [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD] `granted-format` and [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING] `widening-definition`.
- verify-machine: each conformance run records the actual isolation level, and a run reporting a level BELOW the plug-in's floor fails; a plug-in with floor `inprocess` run at `subprocess` emits no notice event, shows "runs as: subprocess" on the consent screen and carries that level in its status; the contract schema and the library contain no host-specific isolation default.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR], [CXC-PC-SUBPROCESS-RUNNER], [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT]
- inprocess-collision: (Standing: architect ruling of r19; NOT covered by this node's decided status.) A host MUST NOT share a plug-in's in-process module objects between hosts or tenants in one process. Module-level state would leak between tenants, against spec 85's per-tenant rule (CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY). When an in-process load would collide with an import package already loaded in the process by another host, tenant or plug-in, the host RAISES that plug-in's actual level to subprocess (always permitted, since a host may run any level at or above the floor) and the consent screen and status show "runs as: subprocess". If the host does not support subprocess, it refuses, naming the plug-in that holds the package. The "already loaded" clause of import-package-name-rule therefore applies to host-provided, stdlib and the host's own modules; a collision between plug-ins is resolved by raising, not refusing. Provenance: architect-synthesis, composeux-architect 2026-11-26 (r19), on the builder's multi-tenant finding against r18; binds both hosts (Gnomon is subprocess-only and is unaffected).
- inprocess-collision-details: (Standing: architect ruling of r19; NOT covered by this node's decided status.) (r19, architect 2026-11-26, on QA's re-verify questions.) (a) A host declares the isolation levels it supports through the Host protocol attribute `isolation_levels`, a set drawn from {inprocess, subprocess}. A host whose set lacks subprocess refuses on a collision, naming the plug-in that holds the package. (b) A host's OWN earlier load of a plug-in is not a collision: re-enable and upgrade of that plug-in in the same host stay in-process. Only a load by a different host, tenant or plug-in collides. (c) "Per-session" for the csrf-defence token is defined at CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS key `csrf-token-scope` (moved there in r20, audit E4; meaning unchanged).
- consented-level-floor: (Standing: architect ruling of r19; NOT covered by this node's decided status.) (r19, ratified by the architect 2026-11-26 from build 38c8b94.) Each install record stores the "runs as" level that its consent screen showed, and enable NEVER runs the plug-in below that level. A plug-in consented at subprocess therefore stays at subprocess even after a colliding holder releases the package. A host whose `isolation_levels` lacks inprocess runs every plug-in at subprocess, and the consent screen says so. Binds both hosts.
- unsupported-floor-refused: (Standing: architect ruling of r19; NOT covered by this node's decided status.) (r19, ratified by the architect 2026-11-26 from build 45bef44.) If a plug-in's `isolation_floor` requires a level that the host's `isolation_levels` lacks (for example a subprocess floor on an in-process-only host), install is refused, naming the host's levels. A host never runs a plug-in at a level it does not support, and never below the plug-in's floor. Binds both hosts.

