## 13. The hello-world fixture and the conformance script

### [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1] hello-world v1: the conformance fixture

- claim: `domain: hello_world`, declaring BOTH hosts. Config: `greeting: str = "hello"`. Publishes STATE `plugins/hello_world/greeting` (the greeting text). Subscribes EVENT `plugins/hello_world/ping`. Publishes EVENT `plugins/hello_world/pong` with `{greeting, n}`. Reports `degraded("empty greeting")` if `greeting == ""`.
- default: `greeting` is `"hello"`.
- n-definition: `n` is the COUNT OF PONGS SENT SINCE THIS START, beginning at 1; a restart (stop then start) resets it to 1. Ruled architect-synthesis, ruled 2026-11-26 at ratify (pre-integration q10).
- boundary-case: `greeting == ""` MUST yield `degraded("empty greeting")`, and the plug-in keeps running.
- manifest-fields-carried: the v1 manifest carries the optional `issue_tracker` and `codeowners` fields so every optional field except `standard_questions` and `permissions` is exercised ([CXC-PC-RULE-SIZE]); it ships no `upgrade` block (reserved), no `standard_questions` (not needed) and no `permissions` (it declares none, so its conformance is unchanged in r21; `permissions` is exercised by the tests of [CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS] and [CXC-PC-NC-MALFORMED-PERMISSION]).
- verify-machine: conformance steps 1 to 5; three pings after one start yield pongs with `n` = 1, 2, 3; after disable and re-enable the next pong has `n` = 1; with `greeting: ""` health is `degraded("empty greeting")`.
- verify-human: none beyond the Plugin Manager glass checks in the script.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — a paraphrase, not a quotation: HoP's order is hello-world first (his words are quoted at [CXC-PC-PROVING-ORDER]); the concrete fixture definition is the architect's.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-HELLO-V2], [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT], [CXC-PC-RULE-SIZE]

### [CXC-PC-HELLO-V2] hello-world v2: exercises migration and re-consent

- claim: v2 ADDS config `repeat: int = 1` (a new field with a default, so it exercises migration) and ADDS subscribe EVENT `plugins/hello_world/name` (so it WIDENS permissions and exercises re-consent).
- default: `repeat` is `1`, which preserves v1 behaviour exactly.
- repeat-definition: `repeat` is the number of pongs published PER PING, each with its own incrementing `n`. Ruled architect-synthesis, ruled 2026-11-26 at ratify (pre-integration q10).
- verify-machine: conformance step 6; with `repeat: 3` one ping yields three pongs with `n` = 1, 2, 3 (then 4, 5, 6 on the next ping); with the default `repeat: 1` behaviour equals v1.
- verify-human: the re-consent screen shows the added subscribe.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UPGRADE]

### [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT] The conformance script: the 9-step lifecycle, run once per host per isolation level

- rule: the script MUST run these steps, in order, once per host and per isolation level: (1) install v1 with the consent screen; (2) set a config value; (3) start; (4) health `running`; (5) publish and subscribe round-trip (a `ping` yields a `pong` carrying the configured greeting); (6) upgrade to v2, which shows re-consent with the added subscribe, and the stored config migrates (`greeting` kept, `repeat` = 1); (7) disable; (8) re-enable; (9) uninstall, with the retained topic tombstoned and the consent record kept.
- matrix: the script runs hello-world at EVERY isolation level the host supports. ComposeIO runs it in ComposeServer at the `inprocess` level AND at the `subprocess` level. Gnomon runs it at whatever levels its spec 50 (revised) supports. The contract fixes no level per host ([CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE]).
- ownership: the conformance script is OWNED by composeux-architect; gnomon-architect is its REVIEWER.
- also-at-subprocess-level: during every subprocess-level run, on whichever host, the local-only assertion of [CXC-PC-RUNNER-LOCAL-ONLY] is checked; the negative controls of [CXC-PC-NEGATIVE-CONTROLS] are run on each host.
- ships-with: the library ([CXC-PC-LIBRARY]); BOTH hosts run it against their pinned contract version ([CXC-PC-CONTRACT-AUTHORITATIVE]).
- verify-machine: the script exits non-zero on any failed step and prints, per step, the observed value (not just pass/fail); each run records host, isolation level, contract version and step results.
- verify-machine: (r24) a stub target whose `host_section` returns `{"probe": 1}` for each fixture id it is called with (`hello-world-v1`, `hello-world-v2`, `NC1` to `NC8`): every manifest the runner installs, at every step or control that installs, carries exactly `{"probe": 1}` at `hosts.<target.host.name>`, and the consented `manifest_sha256` is the digest of the manifest with that value written.
- verify-machine: (r24) a stub target with no `host_section`: every manifest the runner installs carries exactly `{}` at `hosts.<target.host.name>`. ComposeIO's target provides no `host_section` (so its section stays `{}`) and provides `foreign_host = "gnomon"`; with those it passes steps 1 to 9 and the negative controls.
- verify-machine: (r24) the NC4 positive control writes its unknown key at `hosts.<target.foreign_host>`, never at a literal `hosts.gnomon` and never in this host's own section; on ComposeIO (`foreign_host` "gnomon") and on Gnomon (`foreign_host` "composeio") the plug-in installs and the key is ignored.
- verify-machine: (r24) a stub target with no `foreign_host` makes the runner refuse before step 1 with "foreign_host missing"; one whose `foreign_host` equals `target.host.name`, or is `"Bad-Name"` or `"bus"`, makes it refuse before step 1 with a message naming the value; in each case no fixture is installed.
- verify-human: each host's operator watches steps 1, 6 and 9 on the Plugin Manager and confirms the consent screens and the tombstone.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the 9-step lifecycle is from gnomon-architect's brief; the per-level matrix, ownership and reviewer are the architect's; full provenance in the r23 revision record. (r24) `host-section-hook`, `foreign-host` and the four r24 verify-machine lines are architect-synthesis: gnomon-architect's finding and proposal (gnomon-architect message, 2026-11-26: the shared fixtures carry `"hosts": {"composeio": {}, "gnomon": {}}` and NC4's positive control puts an unknown key under `hosts.gnomon`, and Gnomon's strict host-section validator, Gnomon spec 50 R-SECTION at schema_version 5, refuses both), adopted by composeux-architect's ruling under [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS], each host owning its own section.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-HELLO-V1], [CXC-PC-HELLO-V2], [CXC-PC-NEGATIVE-CONTROLS], [CXC-PC-HOST-ISOLATION-CHOICE]
- host-section-hook: (r24, architect-synthesis) The conformance TARGET (the object the conformance factory returns) MAY provide `host_section(fixture: str) -> dict`, returning THIS host's own valid section VALUE for the named fixture. `fixture` is one of the conformance script's own fixture ids: `hello-world-v1`, `hello-world-v2`, and each negative-control fixture by its NC id (`NC1` to `NC8`). The runner writes that value into the fixture's manifest at `hosts.<target.host.name>` before each step or control that installs (so the consented `manifest_sha256` covers it). A target that does not provide `host_section` gets `{}` there; ComposeIO's target provides none, so its section stays `{}`, unchanged. The shared fixtures do not define any host's section content: each host owns its own section ([CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS] rule 3).
- foreign-host: (r24, architect-synthesis) EVERY host's conformance target MUST provide `foreign_host: str`, a host name other than `target.host.name`; ComposeIO's target provides `foreign_host = "gnomon"`. The positive control paired with [CXC-PC-NC-UNKNOWN-CORE-KEY] puts an unknown key inside `hosts.<foreign_host>` and asserts that this host ignores it and the plug-in installs. The runner REFUSES to run before step 1: an absent `foreign_host` with the message "foreign_host missing"; one that equals `target.host.name` or breaks [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS] `host-name-rule` with a message naming the value.
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) The script runs NC9 and NC10 after NC8, and the fixture ids that `host_section` receives gain `NC9` and `NC10`; NC3 sends `"0.4"` (CXC-PC-V03-READS-0-2-UNCHANGED). Steps 1 to 9 and the 0.2 fixtures are unchanged.

### [CXC-PC-NEGATIVE-CONTROLS] The negative controls: each MUST fail loudly, never pass or half-apply

- claim: eight negative controls, each paired with the positive step whose pass it could otherwise only re-assert (see [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE], `pairs-with-a-negative-control`): [CXC-PC-NC-TAMPERED-MANIFEST], [CXC-PC-NC-CLASS-MANIFEST-DRIFT], [CXC-PC-NC-CONTRACT-MISMATCH], [CXC-PC-NC-UNKNOWN-CORE-KEY], [CXC-PC-NC-INVALID-CONFIG], [CXC-PC-NC-WIDENING-REFUSED], [CXC-PC-NC-HOST-PROVIDED-REQUIREMENT] (the seventh is Plumb's R3 condition, accepted by the architect), [CXC-PC-NC-MALFORMED-PERMISSION] (the eighth, r21).
- rule: "loudly" means a named, human-readable refusal or park reason; a silent no-op, a crash of the host, or a half-applied change is a FAIL of the control.
- verify-machine: a mutation check: for each control, remove the guard it tests in a scratch copy of the host and assert the control then FAILS (so the control can distinguish the failure mode it exists for).
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- status-detail: proposed — awaiting ratify
- provenance: architect-synthesis — six of the seven controls are from the narrative; the seventh is Plumb's R3 condition.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONFORMANCE-SCRIPT], [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE]
- v0-3-delta: (0.3) Two more controls join the eight, so the claim above reads ten from 0.3: NC9 CXC-PC-V03-NC-UPGRADE-DECLINED and NC10 CXC-PC-V03-NC-GRANT-BEARING-CONFIG.

