### A declared EULA is part of the consent content, and the library's consent screen shows its text unaltered before the accept and refuse buttons [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-DISPLAY]

- rule: (1) at prepare time the library reads the file named by `eula` and puts it in the consent content as `ConsentContent.eula`, an object `{path, text, sha256}`; the member is `None` when no `eula` is declared. (2) `text` is the file's bytes decoded as UTF-8 with NO change: no newline translation, no trimming, no tab expansion. (3) The library's web consent screen renders it as a block headed "License agreement (EULA) declared by the plug-in", the text inside a `<pre>` with HTML escaping and nothing else done to it, then the line "EULA sha256: <hex>"; the block sits after the permission lines and before the accept and refuse buttons; the text is shown in full, never truncated. (4) One accept covers the permissions and the EULA together. (6) `ConsentContent.text()`, the plain-text rendering that text-only hosts (ComposeIO's CLI, for one) print, includes the EULA block: the heading, the text verbatim, the line "EULA sha256: <hex>" and, on a re-consent, the line "[ADDED] EULA changed"; with no EULA its output is unchanged from 0.1. (5) A host that offers consent (an accept) for a manifest that declares `eula` MUST show exactly `content.eula.text`, in full, on that screen (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HOST-OBLIGATION); any host that shows a EULA shows exactly that text.
- host-obligation: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (CXC-PC-V02-RULING-EULA-HOST-DISPLAY): the MUST of item 5 applies to every host, not only the library's web manager; its requirement and test are CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HOST-OBLIGATION.
- crlf-in-browsers: the bytes the web screen sends are the EULA text with `html.escape` only, CR LF included. A browser's HTML parser normalises CR LF to LF in the DOM, so on the glass the line breaks match the file while the DOM text lacks the carriage returns. The bytes are exact; the helper and the tests compare the bytes sent, not the DOM.
- default: no `eula` declared: no block, no hash line, and the consent content is identical to 0.1's.
- boundary-case: the EULA text contains `<script>`, `&`, quotes and CRLF: the page shows them as characters and executes nothing.
- verify-machine: render a prompt for a package whose EULA holds `<b>&amp;</b>`, tabs, CRLF and U+2028: the screen's `<pre>` text, HTML-unescaped, equals the file's decoded text exactly; the block's byte offset precedes the first accept button and follows the last permission line; a 200 KiB EULA appears in full; `ConsentContent.text()` for the same prompt contains the heading, the exact text, the hash line and (on an upgrade whose EULA changed) the `[ADDED] EULA changed` line, and for hello-world v1 is unchanged from 0.1; with no `eula`, the rendered consent content for hello-world v1 is byte-identical to the 0.1 library's, except for the page footer's contract version (CXC-PC-V02-STORES-COMPAT `page-identity`).
- verify-human: an operator reads a EULA on the screen: all of it is visible by scrolling, line breaks are as in the file, and the hash line is present.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V02-T-EULA-RENDERED-VERBATIM]
- evidence: PASS at composeio main e5c82fb000b8be82e0f14e4f4820da40736ea954 (PR #17, tag plugin-contract-v0.2, compose-plugins 0.2.0): python/compose_plugins/tests/test_v02_license_eula.py: test_CXC_PC_V02_T_EULA_RENDERED_VERBATIM, test_CXC_PC_V02_T_EULA_RENDERED_VERBATIM_absent_is_0_1; the compose_plugins full suite at that commit: 1359 passed, 0 skipped, rc 0 (raw output sha256 d1fc46f1ce473da0f30ddab1894dd8a39ce5e3f32fd5a68025beec1eaaf52996; it counts these tests and does not list them by name); independent candidate review at that commit, round 2: PASS_WITH_NOTES. Filled 2026-11-27 from the build, review and push reports.
- evidence-fills-with: the rendered HTML of the test run, the library commit and the sha256 of the HTML file
- decision-source: DEC-PLUGIN-EULA-IS-A-MANIFEST-CORE-FIELD-2026-11-27 (shown verbatim by any host that shows a EULA)
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided — ratified by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27
