### An upgrade whose EULA differs from the EULA the installed version was consented to requires fresh consent, even when no permission widens [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT]

- rule: let I be the installed record's `eula_sha256` (absent means none) and N the new package's EULA digest (absent means none). The upgrade requires fresh consent from the gate when N is present and N differs from I. It does NOT require it when N is absent (a removed EULA narrows) or N equals I. This trigger is independent of `widening-definition` in CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING, which is unchanged: the gate runs when EITHER holds, and runs ONCE, writing ONE record, when both hold.
- covers: an install record written by a 0.1 library has no `eula_sha256`, so the first upgrade of it to a package that declares `eula` requires consent.
- screen: the re-consent screen shows the new EULA block (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-DISPLAY), marked with the same ADDED marker the screen uses for an added grant, and a line "EULA changed". `ConsentContent` gains `eula_changed: bool` (default False).
- not-a-trigger: `license` never triggers consent.
- refusal: a refused re-consent leaves the installed version, its config, its enabled or disabled state and its install record unchanged and writes a `refused` consent record that carries the NEW digest (as at CXC-PC-NC-WIDENING-REFUSED).
- default: no EULA on either side: the upgrade follows CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING unchanged.
- verify-machine: the table (I, N) to gate-called: (none, none) no; (none, A) yes; (A, A) no; (A, B) yes; (A, none) no; with a permission widening AND a changed EULA: exactly one gate call and one record; a `license`-only change: zero gate calls; a refused (A, B) upgrade leaves `installs.json` byte-identical, leaves the plug-in running if it was, and writes one `refused` record with `eula_sha256` equal to the digest of B.
- verify-human: the operator sees the changed EULA marked as added on the re-consent screen and can read it in full.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V02-T-EULA-RECONSENT-TABLE]
