### `record_decline` refuses a prompt this manager did not prepare, a prompt whose subject has changed since it was prepared, and a prompt it has already declined, and each refusal writes nothing [CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE-PROMPT-VALIDITY]

- prepared: on every `prepare_install` and `prepare_upgrade` the manager records an issue entry `{token, kind, domain, version, manifest_sha256, eula_sha256, fingerprint}` and the returned `ConsentPrompt` carries the token in a private field that is excluded from repr and comparison. `record_decline` raises `PromptNotPrepared` ("record_decline REFUSED: the prompt was not prepared by this manager") when: the argument is not a `ConsentPrompt`; the token is absent or unknown to THIS manager instance (a hand-built prompt, a prompt issued by another manager instance even on the same state root); or `kind`, `domain`, `version`, `manifest_sha256` or `eula_sha256` of the argument differs from its issue entry (a copy made with `dataclasses.replace` and a changed field).
- fresh: the fingerprint is the tuple (state, version, manifest_sha256, event_seq) of the domain's install record at prepare time, or the single value "none" when the domain has no record. `record_decline` raises `PromptStale` ("record_decline REFUSED: the prompt is stale: <reason>") when the domain's fingerprint now differs (reason "the install state of <domain> changed since the prompt was prepared"). Any transition of that domain makes an earlier prompt stale; the host prepares again.
- single-use: a successful `record_decline` marks the issue entry consumed; a second call with the same prompt raises `PromptStale` with reason "the prompt was already declined".
- serialised: the manager holds ONE lock per domain (a re-entrant lock) that `install`, `upgrade`, `uninstall`, `enable`, `disable` and `record_decline` each take for their whole run; the staleness check and the append happen inside it. So no state-changing operation of this manager interleaves between the check and the append, and a decline can never be written after an upgrade has consumed the prompt it describes. The lock is per manager instance: two processes on one state root are outside this rule, as in 0.1 where there is one manager per host instance.
- bounded: the manager keeps at most 1024 issue entries and evicts the oldest; a prompt whose entry was evicted is refused as not prepared.
- default: none.
- boundary-case: a prompt prepared for install, then the plug-in installed by another call, is stale; a prompt prepared for upgrade, then the plug-in disabled, is stale; a prompt prepared for upgrade, then the plug-in uninstalled, is stale.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-UNPREPARED-REFUSED (every not-prepared case, nothing written), CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-STALE-REFUSED (every stale case including single-use and eviction, nothing written), CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-CONCURRENT-WITH-UPGRADE (200 randomised interleavings of a decline and an upgrade on one domain).
- verify-human: none required — fully machine-observable.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-UNPREPARED-REFUSED], [CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-STALE-REFUSED], [CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-CONCURRENT-WITH-UPGRADE]
- evidence: PASS at composeio main e5c82fb000b8be82e0f14e4f4820da40736ea954 (PR #17, tag plugin-contract-v0.2, compose-plugins 0.2.0): python/compose_plugins/tests/test_v02_record_decline.py: test_CXC_PC_V02_T_RD_UNPREPARED_REFUSED, test_CXC_PC_V02_T_RD_STALE_REFUSED, test_CXC_PC_V02_T_RD_CONCURRENT_WITH_UPGRADE; the compose_plugins full suite at that commit: 1359 passed, 0 skipped, rc 0 (raw output sha256 d1fc46f1ce473da0f30ddab1894dd8a39ce5e3f32fd5a68025beec1eaaf52996; it counts these tests and does not list them by name); independent candidate review at that commit, round 2: PASS_WITH_NOTES. Filled 2026-11-27 from the build, review and push reports.
- evidence-fills-with: raw output of the three tests, the library commit and the sha256 of the output file
- decision-source: DEC-PLUGIN-RECORD-DECLINE-CALL-V0-2-2026-11-27 (refuses a prompt that is not one this manager prepared, or that is stale); Plumb's touchpoint answer (the two extra tests)
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided — ratified by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (what counts as stale, the single-use rule, the per-domain lock and the 1024 bound are the writer's, see CXC-PC-V02-RULING-RECORD-DECLINE-STALENESS)
