### Every install transition is one event line that carries the manifest sha256 now in force and the consent record under which it happened [CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-EVENT]

- keys: an event has EXACTLY these keys: `seq` (integer, from the file); `at` (UTC time, ISO 8601 with a `Z` suffix); `host` (the host's name); `event` (one of `install`, `upgrade`, `enable`, `disable`, `uninstall`, `abort`); `domain`; `from_state`; `to_state`; `version`; `manifest_sha256` (64 lowercase hex); `consent` (the consent record as an object, or null); `consent_basis` (one of `recorded`, `carried`, `none`). An `abort` event also carries `aborts_seq` (integer) and `reason` (the failure's exception CLASS name, matching `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`, at most 200 characters, never a message: CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-FAILURE-TEXT); no other event carries those two.
- one-event-per-snapshot-write: each write of `installs.json` is preceded by exactly one event. The operations that write it, and so the events, are: `install` (from `available` or `uninstalled` to `installed`), `upgrade` (from `installed` or `enabled` to `installed`), `enable` (from `installed` or `disabled` to `enabled`), `disable` (from `enabled` to `disabled`), `uninstall` (from `installed`, `enabled` or `disabled` to `uninstalled`). An upgrade of an enabled plug-in is two events, `upgrade` then `enable`, because the manager re-enables through `enable`. A later operation that writes `installs.json` must add its own event here.
- not-events: a refused consent decision (it is only a consent-store record), `configure` (it writes the config store), a status change such as `parked` or `degraded`, and `record_decline` are not transitions and write no event.
- version-and-sha: `version` and `manifest_sha256` are those of the install in force after the transition; for `uninstall` they are those of the version removed.
- consent: `recorded` means `consent` is the consent record that this same operation appended (an `install`, and an `upgrade` whose gate ran for a widening or a changed EULA); it equals that line of the consent store when re-serialised. `carried` means no record was written by this operation and `consent` is the most recent `granted` record for the domain in the consent store at append time (an `upgrade` that did not widen, `enable`, `disable`, `uninstall`); its `manifest_sha256` may differ from the event's, and that difference is the honest statement that the consent predates this manifest. `none` means no granted record exists (consent null). `abort` events carry null and `none`.
- carried-reads-the-consent-store: a `carried` basis reads consent.jsonl at the transition. A consent store with an unreadable line therefore makes `enable`, `disable`, `uninstall` and a non-widening `upgrade` FAIL LOUDLY (`InstallLogRefused`, whose cause class is `ConsentRefused`), nothing changed: the transition fails closed because the consent it would carry cannot be read. In 0.1 those operations never read the consent store. This is the one case where a bad file in the state root stops a transition; a bad `installs.jsonl` never does (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-MALFORMED-LINES).
- consent-before-event: the gate's consent record is appended before the event, so the event can carry it.
- consent-survives-a-failed-append: for `install` and `upgrade` the gate's consent record is appended before the event, so when the event's append fails a consent record already exists. That record is a record of the DECISION, not of an outcome: `installs.json` is byte-identical, nothing is stopped or placed, and the consent record stays (consent records are append-only and are kept even after uninstall). The private `_gate` returns the `ConsentRecord` it appended, so that the install event can carry it; no public signature changes.
- verify-machine: run the 9-step conformance lifecycle at either isolation level and read the log: the events are, in order, `install`, `enable`, `upgrade`, `enable`, `disable`, `enable`, `uninstall`, with `seq` 1 to 7; the first `install` has `from_state` "available" and `consent_basis` "recorded"; the v2 `upgrade` has `consent_basis` "recorded" and its `consent.manifest_sha256` equals its own `manifest_sha256` (the added subscribe widened); the `uninstall` has `to_state` "uninstalled" and `manifest_sha256` equal to v2's; a non-widening upgrade has `consent_basis` "carried" and a `consent.manifest_sha256` different from the event's; every non-null `consent` equals a line of consent.jsonl; a refused upgrade adds a consent line and NO event; each event's key set is exactly as listed.
- verify-human: an operator reads the log of a plug-in's lifecycle and can say who consented to what, and when each version was installed, enabled and removed.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V02-T-INSTALL-LOG-EVENTS-OF-THE-LIFECYCLE]
