### Log text about a failure carries the exception CLASS only, never its message [CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-FAILURE-TEXT]

- rule: every string the library writes into `installs.jsonl`, into an `InstallLogFinding`, or into the message of an `InstallLogRefused`, about a failure, carries the failure's exception class name (`type(exc).__name__`) and NEVER `str(exc)`, `exc.args`, a traceback or any text taken from the failure. In particular an `abort` event's `reason` is exactly the class name of the exception that stopped the operation (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-EVENT `keys`), and an `unreadable` finding names the class of the read error. The exception that propagates to the caller is the original, unchanged, with its own message.
- why: an exception message can carry a path, a secret, a manifest value or text an author controls; the log is a long-lived audit file and its findings are shown on a web page and read by agents.
- default: none.
- boundary-case: an exception whose message contains a newline, a U+2028, a path and a planted marker string; an exception whose class name is not an identifier-shaped string cannot occur because Python class names are identifiers.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V02-T-INSTALL-LOG-FAILURE-TEXT: make an operation fail after its append with an exception whose message is `PLANTED-7f3a9c path=/x/y secret=hunter2` and a custom class `PlantedError`: the `abort` event's `reason` equals `PlantedError`; the string `PLANTED-7f3a9c` appears nowhere in `installs.jsonl`, in any finding message, or in any `InstallLogRefused` message; the exception the caller catches is the original `PlantedError` with its full message; the same holds when the log append itself fails with an `OSError` whose message holds the marker.
- verify-human: none required — fully machine-observable.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V02-T-INSTALL-LOG-FAILURE-TEXT]
