## 17. v0.2 rulings (DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27)

### Ruled: the refusal record keeps the eight 0.1 keys and does not carry `kind` [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-RECORD-KIND]

- question: the record_decline decision says the call appends a refusal record "carrying the prompt's manifest sha256 and kind, through the same ConsentStore append that _gate uses". The consent record has EIGHT keys and none is `kind`, and `_gate` writes none, so "same append, same record" and "carries kind" cannot both hold without adding a key. Does the record gain an optional `kind` (install or upgrade)?
- rule: NO. Keep eight keys. `kind` selects the prompt's checks and the error text, and is not written. Reason: a new key on every record (gate-written ones too, or the two writers would differ in shape) would make every 0.2 consent line unreadable to a 0.1 library, which turns any roll-back of a host's pin into a store the old library refuses; the install-versus-upgrade distinction is recoverable from the domain's history in installs.jsonl and from the version.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE (the `writes` key lists no `kind`), CXC-PC-V02-STORES-COMPAT (`consent-jsonl`), CXC-PC-V02-CHG-05
- if-reopened: add `kind` (one of `install`, `upgrade`) to `optional-keys` in CXC-PC-V02-CHG-05 and to `record-key` handling in CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HASH-RECORD; add it to the `writes` key of CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE; make `_gate` write it too; extend test 1 to assert it; and change the known limit of CXC-PC-V02-STORES-COMPAT so that EVERY 0.2 record is unreadable by 0.1.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: a host needs the kind inside the consent store, not recoverable from the log, for an audit it already runs
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD]

### Ruled: the EULA hash is an optional consent-record key, not a grant string [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-EULA-HASH-PLACEMENT]

- question: the decision says the sha256 is "stored in the consent record" and that a changed EULA forces fresh consent "like a widened permission". It does not say where in the record. Two readings: (A) an optional record key `eula_sha256` plus a separate re-consent trigger; (B) a sixth grant string `eula:sha256:<hex>` in `granted`, so the existing subset test forces re-consent with no new trigger.
- rule: (A). Reason: (B) needs the exact text of `granted-format` (the guarded key that holds the isolation-floor clause, area-05) to change from "exactly five string forms" and needs `eula` added to the reserved host names of `host-name-rule`; touching a guarded key needs HoP's word. (A) touches no guarded key. (B) is the less code; (A) is the one that needs no one's word.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HASH-RECORD, CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT, CXC-PC-V02-CHG-05, CXC-PC-V02-GUARD-CHECK
- if-reopened: rewrite the two nodes named above; edit `granted-format` and `host-name-rule` as a change node that HoP has agreed to; drop `optional-keys` and `eula-reconsent` from CXC-PC-V02-CHG-05.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: the guard's area-05 and area-07 are read as not covering a new grant form
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HASH-RECORD], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD], [CXC-PC-V0-2-GROWTH-GUARD]

### Ruled: a host that offers consent for a manifest declaring `eula` MUST show it [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-EULA-HOST-DISPLAY]

- question: the decision says "shown verbatim by any host that shows a EULA". It does not say a host must show one. If a host offers accept for such a manifest without showing the text, the record's `eula_sha256` states a consent to a document the operator never saw.
- rule: YES, MUST. A host whose consent screen offers accept for a manifest that declares `eula` MUST show `content.eula.text`; a host that cannot refuses to install such a manifest, naming `eula`. The library's own web screen already does (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-DISPLAY item 3).
- consequence: the MUST is a new host obligation, so Gnomon's spec 50 R-CONSENT is audited against it; gnomon-architect already shows a EULA screen when one is declared.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-EULA-DISPLAY (item 5 of `rule`) and CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HOST-OBLIGATION (the requirement and its test); CXC-PC-V02-CHG-06 (a pointer key on CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES).
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: a host exists whose consent is given out of band (a signed policy) and needs no screen
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-DISPLAY]

### Ruled: the `license` grammar and the EULA size and content limits stand as drafted [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-FIELD-LIMITS]

- question: the decisions fix neither a validation depth for `license` nor any limit for `eula`. This delta chose: `license` shape-checked (SPDX 2.3 Annex D grammar), 1 to 256 ASCII characters, with no look-up in the SPDX License List; `eula` 1 byte to 256 KiB, UTF-8, no BOM, no Cc other than tab, line feed and carriage return, no bidirectional controls, not blank, no symlink. Are these the numbers and sets the owner wants?
- rule: keep them. 256 KiB mirrors the manifest's own cap so no one number is a new order of magnitude; the bidirectional set is the published Trojan Source set; the shape-only licence check cannot go stale.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD, CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD
- if-reopened: change the number or set in the named node's rule key, boundary-case list and accepted examples, and in its test table; nothing else depends on them.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: a real plug-in ships a EULA over 256 KiB, or a host wants licence ids checked against a list
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-LICENSE-FIELD], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-FIELD]

### Ruled: a manifest stating contract 0.1 that carries `license` or `eula` is refused by a 0.2 host [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-VERSIONED-KEYS]

- question: spec 95 says a manifest is read by the contract it states, but it does not say what a 0.2 host does with a manifest that states 0.1 and carries a 0.2 key. Refuse (the key is not defined by the contract the manifest names), or accept (the host is 0.2 and understands it)?
- rule: refuse, naming the key. Accepting makes the stated `contract` a lie that a 0.2 host quietly corrects, and a 0.1 host would then refuse the same file for a reason the author can't see on the 0.2 host.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-VERSION-0-2 rule (3), CXC-PC-V02-CHG-03
- if-reopened: delete rule (3) and the two refusal rows of its test; add the sentence that a 0.2 host accepts the two keys whatever `contract` says.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: a tool that generates 0.1 manifests starts emitting `license` before authors move to 0.2
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-VERSION-0-2], [CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE]

### Ruled: a prompt is stale when its domain's install fingerprint changed or it was already declined [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-RECORD-DECLINE-STALENESS]

- question: the decision says the call refuses a stale prompt and does not define it. This delta defines it as: the domain's install fingerprint (state, version, manifest_sha256, event_seq) differs from the one at prepare time, or the prompt was already declined; and adds a per-domain lock and a 1024-entry bound on issued prompts. Is that the intended strictness?
- rule: keep it. The strict form means any transition of the domain makes the prompt stale, so a host prepares again after a disable it did not intend to be relevant; the cost is one re-prepare, the gain is that a decline record can never describe a state that no longer exists.
- host-read: gnomon-architect, 2026-11-27: the strictness is fine. Gnomon's prompt identity is a subset of the fingerprint, it re-prepares inside its operator lock, and it maps PromptStale and PromptNotPrepared to HTTP 409.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE-PROMPT-VALIDITY, CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-STALE-REFUSED
- if-reopened: loosen the fingerprint (for example (state-allows-the-operation, version, manifest_sha256), ignoring `event_seq`) in the named node and test; the lock and the single-use rule may stay.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: a host holds a prompt across normal enable and disable actions and re-preparing would break its flow
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE-PROMPT-VALIDITY]

### Ruled: the install log is write-ahead with abort events and report-only findings, and never blocks [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-INSTALL-LOG-FAILURE-POLICY]

- question: the request says the log is written BEFORE the snapshot is rewritten. That makes a dangling event possible (a failure or a crash after the append). This delta handles it with an `abort` event on failure and a report-only divergence check after a crash, and never blocks. Is that the intended failure policy, or should a divergence block further transitions of that domain until a person clears it?
- rule: report only, never block. The snapshot, not the log, is what the manager runs on; blocking would let a crash at the wrong instant lock an operator out of a working plug-in; the finding is visible on the front page.
- extended-by: gnomon-architect's host-side read of 2026-11-27, ratified in the same decision: report-only also covers a malformed log line (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-MALFORMED-LINES), log text about a failure carries the exception class only (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-FAILURE-TEXT), and the findings are a method (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-FINDINGS-METHOD).
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-WRITE-ORDER, CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-DIVERGENCE
- if-reopened: if blocking: add to the divergence node that `enable`, `disable`, `upgrade` and `uninstall` of a domain with a finding raise a named refusal until the finding is gone, and say what clears one (a person appends an `abort` through a documented tool). If the abort event is not wanted: drop the `abort` event and `aborts_seq` and `reason`, and accept that a failed operation leaves an unmarked dangling event.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: the log is meant as the source of truth and the snapshot a cache, in which case the log must carry enough to rebuild it
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-WRITE-ORDER], [CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-DIVERGENCE]

### Ruled: an uninstall leaves no consent record, and record_decline takes install and upgrade prompts only [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-UNINSTALL-RECORD]

- question: spec 95's gate says every uninstall "waits for HoP" on Gnomon, and the record rule says every consent decision produces a record. The library has no uninstall prompt, record_decline takes only install and upgrade prompts, and the web confirm writes no consent record. Is an uninstall decision a consent decision that must be recorded?
- rule: NO for 0.2. The uninstall confirm is the authenticated operator's explicit second act, the uninstall itself leaves an `uninstall` event in the log, and a cancelled one changes nothing. A consent record exists to record what was GRANTED to a plug-in; removing one grants nothing.
- host-read: gnomon-architect, 2026-11-27: install and upgrade prompts only is acceptable.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM (key `no-record`), CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE (key `accepts`)
- if-reopened: add an `uninstall` prompt kind to `PluginManager` (a `prepare_uninstall`), have the web confirm and cancel write a record through it, and extend record_decline to accept it; the consent record would then need a way to say it is an uninstall, which re-opens CXC-PC-V02-RULING-RECORD-KIND.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: an audit must show every uninstall refusal as a consent-store line
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES], [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-RECORD-KIND]

### Ruled: hosts add no lines to the library's uninstall confirm [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-UNINSTALL-HOST-LINES]

- question: a host removes more than the library knows (Gnomon lists jobs, a tab, an allow-list entry, a level, a bus grant and a data-folder choice on its own uninstall screen). Does the library's confirm gain a `Host` hook so that a host's lines appear on it?
- rule: NO in 0.2. The library's confirm is the floor every host gets; Gnomon keeps its own richer screen in front of it. A hook adds a `Host` member, which is the one thing item 3's additive rule and CXC-PC-V02-STORES-COMPAT promise not to do.
- host-read: gnomon-architect, 2026-11-27: no host hook suits; Gnomon keeps its own screen in front of the library's confirm.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM (the `screen` key lists only the library's own lines)
- if-reopened: add an OPTIONAL `Host.uninstall_effects(domain) -> list[str]` (default none), render each string as one more line on the screen, and give it a test; it would remain additive because it is optional.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: Gnomon cannot put its own screen in front of the library's confirm
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM]

### Ruled: build and tag 0.2, and hold any PyPI upload of compose-plugins 0.2.0 until a grant is cited [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-PYPI-UPLOAD-GRANT]

- question: Plumb's touchpoint answer holds a PyPI upload of 0.2.0 until a grant from the operator is cited; the 0.1.0 upload was his own ask and no grant for later versions has been found. Plumb puts the question to him unless the contract owner's definition carries one. Is there one?
- rule: build and tag `plugin-contract-v0.2` and `compose-plugins` 0.2.0 on the private and public repositories as the release gates allow, and do NOT upload to PyPI until a grant is cited. Gnomon's follow-up change waits on the published 0.2 and its pin, so the upload is the gating step for Gnomon, not for this spec.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V02-BASIS (`release-gates`)
- if-reopened: if a standing grant exists, cite it at CXC-PC-V02-BASIS and drop this node.
- answered-by: DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 (every OPEN ruled on its lean)
- invalidating-assumption: the operator has already granted publication of later versions
- status: decided
- ruling: ruled by DEC-SPEC95-V0-2-DELTA-RATIFIED-2026-11-27 on the lean below, which is now the rule; it is applied in the nodes named at `applied-in`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-RELEASE-TAGS]

### Resolved: the corpus copy of spec 95 (r24 plus a citation repoint) is the intended base [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-BASE-SHA]

- question: the requester gave sha256 1ff2a6ef8555fe780210b48047c8e3eeda103408fcc574ae0fbe8d7a9f9a6a7c for spec 95 r24; the file in the corpus measures 009a23312b167654a935a11dffc370732a36a70021830f27aa6a5cb96158585e. Is the corpus file the intended r24, or was another copy meant?
