## 18. v0.3 additions (freezes the manifest)

### [CXC-PC-V03-BASIS] Contract 0.3 is an additive MINOR over 0.2 that FREEZES the manifest schema; this section is a delta on r25, ratified 2026-11-28 and not yet built

- claim: contract 0.3 changes eight things and no more: consent at grant, EULA, capability and warning changes only, refuse always stops, an operator-attributed lifecycle record, an advisory screen that flags grant-bearing config fields, declared `capabilities` and `warnings` with a host policy hook, a keep-or-delete choice for the plug-in's data at uninstall, a web manager that mounts under any prefix, and the freeze of the manifest schema with its compatibility rule. Every node of this section is a requirement or a test of one of them.
- note: Base. spec 95 r25 (contract 0.2) at composeio main 0c6bb2886b711fe78b0cc0e2f56a5d5c6c2c6ce8; library compose-plugins 0.2.0 (tag plugin-contract-v0.2). The release this delta describes is tag `plugin-contract-v0.3` with `compose-plugins` 0.3.0.
- note: Not built. every requirement and test node of this section carries `evidence: EMPTY` and an `evidence-fills-with` line. EMPTY is the sentinel for "never run"; an absent key would mean nobody thought to write it.
- note: How edited. this delta adds sections 18 and 19, relationship lines at the end, and ONE new key on each existing node it touches (`v0-3-delta`, or `evidence-v0-3-delta` on CXC-PC-EVIDENCE); the title, the frontmatter `scope` and `updated`, and the header summary are re-worded; no existing key of any existing node is edited or removed. The list of touched nodes is in the delta record that accompanies this file.
- note: Guard check. none of the 14 areas or 4 hardening checks of CXC-PC-V0-2-GROWTH-GUARD is extended: no `validator-readings`, `granted-format`, `widening-definition`, `host-name-rule`, `permissions-widen` or `code-is-a-permission` text is edited; widening is unchanged; the new manifest fields and the new refusal are gated by the manifest's stated contract (CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3).
- note: Out of scope. Plugin Discovery (listing GitHub releases, summary cards, a token held by reference by the host) is for a later v0.4. Its install rule is recorded here so that 0.3 text does not contradict it: discovery downloads the release file (the package) to the machine and the contract installs from that LOCAL file; declared claims are never enforced.
- note: Two digests, not one. `manifest_sha256` is the SHA-256 of the exact bytes of the shipped `manifest.json` (CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL `manifest-sha256-definition`) and is already recorded at install. The install under Discovery also records the sha256 of the DOWNLOADED RELEASE FILE itself (the package, for example the wheel); that is a different digest, taken over different bytes, and it is a v0.4 addition to the install record or the log. It is not a manifest field, so the freeze is untouched, and nothing in 0.3 records it.
- note: Revision 2026-11-28. The frontmatter `confidentiality` of this file changes from `internal-only` to `public`: the file is already on public main and the public authoring guide cites it.
- rule: Fix upstream. a Plugin Manager capability gap is fixed UPSTREAM in compose-plugins, in the shared library, and never worked around in a host.
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the 0.3.0 release commit, the full suite output and its sha256, and the independent review verdict
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-28 by composeux-architect, contract owner
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the eight items are the contract owner's direction of 2026-11-28; the node shapes are the writer's
- basis: owner-decided (project owner)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V0-2-GROWTH-GUARD], [CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE], [CXC-PC-RELEASE-TAGS], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-INSTALL], [CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3], [CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS], [CXC-PC-V03-REFUSE-ALWAYS-STOPS], [CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD], [CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG], [CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS], [CXC-PC-V03-UNINSTALL-DATA-CHOICE], [CXC-PC-V03-MOUNTABLE-WEB-MANAGER], [CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN]

### [CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3] A 0.3 host reads 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 manifests; `capabilities` and `warnings` and the grant-free config advisory apply only to a manifest that says `contract: "0.3"`

- rule: (1) the library reports its contract as "0.3". (2) A 0.3 host accepts a manifest whose `contract` is "0.1", "0.2" or "0.3", by rule (4) of CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE. (3) A manifest that states "0.1" or "0.2" and carries `capabilities` or `warnings` is REFUSED at install naming the key and stating "defined from contract 0.3", because a manifest is read by the contract it states (the same reading as CXC-PC-V02-VERSION-0-2 rule (3)). (4) The grant-free config advisory (CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG) is emitted only for a manifest that states "0.3"; a manifest that states "0.1" or "0.2" is validated exactly as under 0.2. (5) A 0.2 host refuses a "0.3" manifest by CXC-PC-NC-CONTRACT-MISMATCH, unchanged. (6) The library ships a third schema file, `manifest-0.3.schema.json`, reporting `x-compose-plugins-contract: "0.3"`; `manifest-0.1.schema.json` and `manifest-0.2.schema.json` stay byte-identical to the files shipped with 0.2.0; the schema is selected by the manifest's own `contract` (CXC-PC-V02-SCHEMA-FILES). (7) The tag is `plugin-contract-v0.3`, cut with `compose-plugins` 0.3.0.
- code-read: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/version.py line 15 reads `HOST_CONTRACT = "0.2"`; python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/manifest.py line 973 reads `SCHEMA_RESOURCES = {"0.1": "manifest-0.1.schema.json", "0.2": "manifest-0.2.schema.json"}`. Both change; `check_contract` in version.py already implements rule (2) for any host constant.
- consequence: Negative control input. CXC-PC-NC-CONTRACT-MISMATCH names `"0.3"` on a 0.2 host. From 0.3 the conformance script MUST send the host's MINOR plus one, `"0.4"`, so the control still tests a manifest newer than the host; sending `"0.3"` to a 0.3 host would install it and fail the control.
- default: a manifest that carries none of the new keys behaves exactly as under 0.2, whichever of the three `contract` values it states.
- boundary-case: `contract: "0.2"` with `capabilities` is refused naming `capabilities`; `contract: "0.1"` with `warnings` is refused naming `warnings`; `contract: "0.4"` is refused naming `0.4` and `0.3`.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-T-VERSION-TABLE.
- verify-human: none required — structural.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-T-VERSION-TABLE]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the raw output of the table-driven test with the library commit and the sha256 of the output file
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-28 by composeux-architect, contract owner
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the MINOR rule is the contract's; applying the manifest-states-its-own-version reading to the new keys follows CXC-PC-V02-VERSION-0-2
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-VERSION-RULE], [CXC-PC-V02-VERSION-0-2], [CXC-PC-V02-SCHEMA-FILES], [CXC-PC-NC-CONTRACT-MISMATCH], [CXC-PC-RELEASE-TAGS], [CXC-PC-UNKNOWN-KEYS]

### [CXC-PC-V03-T-VERSION-TABLE] Test: the version table accepts the new keys only under "0.3" and refuses "0.4" naming both versions

- procedure: build manifests from the hello-world v1 manifest: (0.2 plain), (0.2 with `capabilities`), (0.2 with `warnings`), (0.3 plain), (0.3 with `capabilities`), (0.3 with `warnings`), (0.3 with both), (0.4 plain); run each through the validator and through install; record the outcome and the refusal text. Add (0.2 with a config_schema property named `path`) and (0.3 with the same): both install; the second carries a named advisory and the first none (CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG).
- asserts: rows 1, 4, 5, 6, 7 and the 0.2 `path` row install; rows 2 and 3 are refused naming the key and "defined from contract 0.3"; row 8 is refused naming `0.4` and `0.3`; the 0.3 `path` row installs and its validation result holds one `config-grant-bearing` advisory naming the property, while the 0.2 row holds none; the library constant equals "0.3" and equals the shipped 0.3 schema's `x-compose-plugins-contract`; the two earlier schema files are byte-identical to their 0.2.0 digests.
- verify-machine: the test exits non-zero if any row's outcome or named key differs.
- verify-human: none required.
- evidence: EMPTY
- status: proposed
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3]

### [CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS] Consent is asked at install and at an upgrade that widens grants or changes the EULA, a capability or a warning, and nowhere else; enable, disable, configure and uninstall take no consent gate

- rule: (1) The host's consent gate runs exactly at: `install`; and `upgrade` when `upgrade_widens(...)` is true (CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING) or the new package's EULA digest differs from the installed record's `eula_sha256` (CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT) or `declarations_change(...)` is true: a NEW or CHANGED `capabilities` token, or a NEW or CHANGED warning (by text sha256), plug-in or host (CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE). (2) `enable`, `disable`, `configure` and `uninstall` call no gate and write no consent record. Each needs the host's authenticated operator instead: through the web manager, the host's auth hook (CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS); through the library, the `operator` argument (CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD-WRITE). (3) An upgrade that satisfies none of the three triggers calls no gate; its refuse path is CXC-PC-V03-REFUSE-ALWAYS-STOPS. (4) A change of `license` or of a permission `purpose` alone is not a trigger; a REMOVED capability or warning is not a trigger either (CXC-PC-V03-RULING-DECLARATION-CHANGE-GATES).
- why: consent is for what a plug-in may do. Enabling, disabling or reconfiguring it changes nothing it was granted, so a consent screen there is noise that trains operators to click through the one that matters.
- code-read: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/lifecycle.py, PluginManager.upgrade, lines 705 to 707: `if eula_changed or upgrade_widens(old["granted"], prompt.content.granted,` ... `record = self._gate(prompt, operator, accepted)  # refused: no change` is the only gate call on the upgrade path; `configure` (lines 552 to 554) is `self._check("configure", domain)` then `self.configs.put(domain, config)` with no gate; `disable` (line 673) and `uninstall` (line 730) take a domain and nothing else. The 0.2 behaviour already matches rules (1) and (2); this node makes them normative and tested.
- default: none.
- boundary-case: an install whose host gate refuses writes a refused consent record and changes nothing (CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD); a refused gate at a widening upgrade leaves v1 in place (CXC-PC-NC-WIDENING-REFUSED).
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-T-GATE-CALLS.
- verify-human: on the glass an operator disables, re-enables, configures and uninstalls a plug-in and sees no consent screen; installs and sees one.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-T-GATE-CALLS]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the raw output of the gate-spy test with the library commit and the sha256 of the output file
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-28 by composeux-architect, contract owner
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the rule is the contract owner's direction of 2026-11-28; the enumeration and the spy test are the writer's
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-GATES], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-DISABLE], [CXC-PC-LIFECYCLE-UPGRADE], [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-DECLARATION-CHANGE-GATES]

### [CXC-PC-V03-T-GATE-CALLS] Test: a spy on the host's consent gate counts its calls across the whole lifecycle

- procedure: run install, configure, enable, disable, enable, a non-widening upgrade with an unchanged EULA, a widening upgrade, an upgrade that changes only the EULA, an upgrade that only adds a capability token, an upgrade that only changes a warning's text, an upgrade that only removes a warning, and uninstall, with a host whose `consent_gate` is a counting spy that grants.
- asserts: the spy is called once at install, zero times at configure, enable, disable, the non-widening upgrade and uninstall, once at the widening upgrade, once at the EULA-only upgrade, once at the capability-only upgrade, once at the warning-text-only upgrade, and zero times at the warning-removal upgrade; the consent store gains one line per gate call and no other line.
- verify-machine: non-zero exit on any count that differs.
- verify-human: none required.
- evidence: EMPTY
- status: proposed
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS]

### [CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE] `declarations_change` is true when an upgrade adds or changes a capability token or a warning, and it forces the consent gate like a changed EULA

- rule: (1) Define `declarations_change(old, new)`. `old` is the installed record's `capabilities` (a sorted list of tokens, absent read as empty) and `warning_digests` (a map from acknowledgement key `<source>:<id>` to the sha256 of that warning's text, absent read as empty). `new` is the same two things for the package being upgraded and for the host's `policy` result (CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK). (2) It is true iff (a) some token of the new `capabilities` is not in the old set, or (b) some acknowledgement key of the new warnings, plug-in or host, is absent from the old map or maps to a different sha256. (3) A removed token or a removed warning never makes it true (narrowing, as in CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING `permissions-widen`); a changed `title` alone does not (only the text is hashed). (4) When true, the gate runs ONCE, together with any widening or EULA trigger, and the consent page shows every current warning for acknowledgement (CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK); a refusal is recorded and stops the upgrade exactly as for a widening upgrade. (5) `install` and `upgrade` write the installed record's `capabilities` and `warning_digests` (omitted when empty), so the next upgrade can compare. A record written by a 0.2 library has neither and reads as empty, so the first 0.3 upgrade of such a plug-in that declares `agent-messaging` asks.
- why: a capability or a warning changes the risk the operator accepted. `agent-messaging` arriving in an upgrade must not install without the host-required warning being shown and acknowledged.
- code-read: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/lifecycle.py lines 703 to 707 form the 0.2 trigger: `eula_changed = ...` then `if eula_changed or upgrade_widens(old["granted"], prompt.content.granted,` `old["runs_as"], prompt.runs_as):` then `record = self._gate(prompt, operator, accepted)`. The 0.3 `if` gains `or declarations_change(...)`. python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/consent.py line 146 defines `upgrade_widens`, the model for this predicate.
- default: no capabilities and no warnings on either side gives false.
- boundary-case: an upgrade that adds `agent-messaging` while the host's policy requires its warning is true even when the plug-in declares no warning of its own; an upgrade that keeps the same token and the same texts is false; a host that edits its own warning text between releases makes it true for every plug-in that declares the token.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-T-UPGRADE-ADDS-AGENT-MESSAGING, and the rows of CXC-PC-V03-T-GATE-CALLS.
- verify-human: on the glass an operator upgrading a plug-in that now declares `agent-messaging` sees the consent page with the host-required warning and must acknowledge it.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-T-UPGRADE-ADDS-AGENT-MESSAGING]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the raw output of the predicate table and of the upgrade test, with the library commit and the sha256 of each output file
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-28 by composeux-architect, contract owner
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the trigger is the contract owner's ruling of 2026-11-28; the predicate, the record keys and the comparison by text digest are the writer's
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-WIDENING], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT], [CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK], [CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK], [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-DECLARATION-CHANGE-GATES]

### [CXC-PC-V03-T-UPGRADE-ADDS-AGENT-MESSAGING] Test: an upgrade that adds `agent-messaging` shows the consent page with the host-required warning, and a decline stops it

- procedure: with a stub host whose `policy` returns one host warning for `agent-messaging`, install a 0.3 plug-in with no capability and no warning; then upgrade to a package that differs only by `capabilities: ["agent-messaging"]` (no widening, no EULA). Run it three times: (a) through the library with the full `acknowledged_warnings` mapping; (b) through the web manager, reading the consent page and then posting accept with every box set; (c) through the web manager, posting refuse; and a fourth run with `accepted=False` and a gate that refuses.
- asserts: in every run the gate is called once and the consent page lists the host-required warning (labelled "required by this host") with its own acknowledgement control; (a) and (b) upgrade, write a consent record carrying `acknowledged_warnings` with `host:<id>` and its digest, and write an `upgrade` entry whose `consent_basis` is `recorded`; (c) and the refusing gate leave v1 installed with `installs.json` and the code tree byte-identical, write a `refused` consent record without `acknowledged_warnings`, and append one `decline` entry; a second upgrade to a package that adds nothing calls no gate.
- verify-machine: non-zero exit on any mismatch.
- verify-human: none required.
- evidence: EMPTY
- status: proposed
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE], [CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK], [CXC-PC-V03-REFUSE-ALWAYS-STOPS]

### [CXC-PC-V03-REFUSE-ALWAYS-STOPS] An operator's refuse or cancel stops any operation, gate or not: an upgrade with no gate and `accepted=False` aborts with no change and writes a `decline` lifecycle entry

- rule: (1) When a gate runs, its decision governs, as in 0.2 (a refusal is recorded and raises `ConsentDenied`). (2) When NO gate runs for an `upgrade` (it satisfies none of the triggers of CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS), `accepted=False` ABORTS the operation before any side effect: it raises the new `OperatorDeclined` (a subclass of `PluginContractRefusal`) with a message of the form "upgrade of '<domain>' <version> DECLINED by the operator; nothing changed"; no consent record is written (no consent was asked), no code is placed, the install record, the stored config, the running plug-in and `installs.json` are byte-identical to before, and the manager appends exactly one lifecycle entry with `event` `decline` and `result` `declined` (CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD). (3) The check precedes the write-ahead append and precedes stopping the running plug-in. (4) The web manager labels the no-gate upgrade screen with the buttons "Upgrade" (`decision=upgrade`) and "Cancel" (`decision=cancel`), never "Accept" and "Refuse"; a screen whose upgrade needs the gate keeps "Accept" (`decision=accept`) and "Refuse" (`decision=refuse`). (5) The web layer treats the operation as accepted only when `decision` is exactly one value and that value is `accept` or `upgrade`; a missing, repeated, `cancel`, `refuse` or unknown value is not accepted. (6) The web layer answers an `OperatorDeclined` with a redirect to the plug-in's detail page, not an error page: a cancel is not a failure. (7) Every other operation that offers the operator a refuse or cancel control keeps stopping on it (the uninstall confirm's `cancel`, CXC-PC-V02-UNINSTALL-CONFIRM).
- why: Defect. registered as DEFECT-COMPOSE-PLUGINS-UPGRADE-THAT-WIDENS-3E963B. In 0.2.0 an upgrade that does not widen and keeps the EULA proceeds whatever the operator chose, so a click on "Refuse" upgrades the plug-in.
- code-read: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/lifecycle.py, PluginManager.upgrade, lines 703 to 707: `eula_changed = eula is not None and eula.sha256 != old.get("eula_sha256")`, `record = None`, `if eula_changed or upgrade_widens(old["granted"], prompt.content.granted,`, `old["runs_as"], prompt.runs_as):`, `record = self._gate(prompt, operator, accepted)  # refused: no change`. `accepted` is read only at line 707, inside the `if`; when the `if` is false it is never read. python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/web.py lines 188 to 189: `m.upgrade(form["source"], consented_sha256=form["sha256"], operator=identity,` and `accepted=form.get("decision") == "accept",` pass a refuse click as `accepted=False`, which the library then ignores. The screen at web.py `_consent_screen` lines 293 to 296 always renders the buttons `("accept", "accept"), ("refuse", "refuse")`.
- default: `accepted` has no default (it is a required keyword of `upgrade`); a caller that omits it gets a `TypeError` as in 0.2.
- boundary-case: `accepted=False` on an upgrade that DOES widen is the existing gate path (the gate decides); `accepted=False` on an upgrade whose domain is not installed is the existing transition refusal; a no-gate upgrade with `accepted=True` proceeds and writes no consent record, as in 0.2.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-NC-UPGRADE-DECLINED.
- verify-human: on the glass an operator opens a non-widening upgrade, sees "Upgrade" and "Cancel", presses "Cancel" and sees the plug-in still at its old version; opens a widening upgrade and still sees "Accept" and "Refuse".
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-NC-UPGRADE-DECLINED]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the raw output of the negative control at both isolation levels with the library commit and the sha256 of the output file
- status: ratified
