### [CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD] Every state-changing operation appends one operator-attributed entry to the append-only `installs.jsonl`; the entry is the 0.2 event with three added keys and two added event names (the record schema; compatibility is CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD-COMPAT)

- claim: Relation to 0.2. the record IS the 0.2 install event log (CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-FILE, CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-EVENT); there is no second file. `installs.jsonl` stays beside `installs.json` in the host's state root, append-only, with no library operation that rewrites or deletes a line. The 0.3 entry maps the requested fields so: the operation is the existing `event` key, the UTC time is the existing `at` key, `domain` and `version` are unchanged, and the operator, the result and the consent-record id are new keys. The library writes every entry; no `Host` member writes one.
- rule: Operations. `event` is one of `install`, `upgrade`, `enable`, `disable`, `uninstall`, `abort` (0.2) and, new in 0.3, `configure` and `decline`. Entries for `configure` and `decline` carry `from_state` equal to `to_state` (the domain's current state; for a declined first install, `available`).
- rule: New keys. every entry written by a 0.3 library carries exactly three more keys beside the 0.2 keys. `operator`: a non-empty one-line string of at most 200 code points (the blank and one-line rules of CXC-PC-MANIFEST-FIELDS `validator-readings` (12)), or null when the caller supplied none (CXC-PC-V03-RULING-OPERATOR-NULL). `result`: `ok` on an `install`, `upgrade`, `enable`, `disable`, `uninstall` or `configure` entry (written ahead, so it states that the operation passed its checks and is proceeding; a later `abort` entry retracts it), `declined` on a `decline` entry, `aborted` on an `abort` entry. `consent_id`: the lowercase hex sha256 of the consent record's line as written to `consent.jsonl` (the UTF-8 bytes of `json.dumps(record.to_json(), sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False)`, no trailing line feed), or null; it is null exactly when `consent` is null (CXC-PC-V03-RULING-CONSENT-ID). An `uninstall` entry carries a fourth key, `data`, one of `kept`, `deleted`, `none` (CXC-PC-V03-UNINSTALL-DATA-CHOICE); no other entry carries it.
- rule: Decline entry. a `decline` entry is written when an operator's refusal stops an `install` or an `upgrade`: at a gate that refused (its `consent` is the refused record just appended, `consent_basis` `recorded`, `consent_id` its digest), and at a no-gate upgrade with `accepted=False` (`consent` null, `consent_basis` `none`, `consent_id` null). Its `version` and `manifest_sha256` are those of the package the operator declined.
- rule: Never carried. no entry carries a config value, a EULA text or a warning text; `configure` records that the config changed, not what it became.
- code-read: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/store.py lines 80 to 81 define `EVENT_KEYS = ("seq", "at", "host", "event", "domain", "from_state", "to_state", "version",` `"manifest_sha256", "consent", "consent_basis")` and line 84 `EVENTS = ("install", "upgrade", "enable", "disable", "uninstall", "abort")`; `_event_problem` (line 100) refuses any key set other than those. lifecycle.py `_event` (line 413) builds the dict with those eleven keys only. consent.py line 477 `ConsentRecord.to_json` is the serialisation `consent_id` hashes.
- default: none.
- boundary-case: an entry whose `operator` is blank, multi-line or longer than 200 code points is not written: the operation raises the existing `InstallLogRefused`, nothing changes, and no entry is appended; a `consent_id` that is not 64 lowercase hex, or is null while `consent` is not (or the reverse), makes the line malformed.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-T-LIFECYCLE-RECORD.
- verify-human: an operator reads the log of a plug-in's lifecycle and can say who installed, configured, enabled, disabled, declined and removed it, and when, from the log alone.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-T-LIFECYCLE-RECORD]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the installs.jsonl and consent.jsonl of a real run of the lifecycle, with the library commit and the sha256 of each file
- status: ratified
