### [CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG] A plug-in's `config_schema` should carry no grant-bearing field; the validator advises with a named warning and never refuses

- rule: (1) Authoring rule: a plug-in's config does not name a bus namespace, a host, a filesystem path or a grant; reach is widened only by upgrade and consent. (2) For a manifest that states `contract: "0.3"`, the validator emits one ADVISORY for each grant-bearing property in its validation result, with the code `config-grant-bearing`, the JSON pointer of the property, the test that matched, and the text "this config field looks grant-bearing". It does NOT refuse the manifest. (3) The consent content of that manifest carries one line per advisory, "config field <pointer> looks grant-bearing (structural check; not verified)"; it needs no acknowledgement and is not a consent trigger. (4) A property is grant-bearing when its NAME, split into tokens, contains a token of the name tokens, or its schema declares a `format` of the formats below. (5) Ordinary values (strings, numbers, integers, booleans, enums, labels, thresholds) whose names and formats match neither list draw nothing.
- rule: Structural author aid. This is a screen of declared names and formats that helps an author notice a mistake. It is not enforcement and not proof, and the contract claims no more: nothing checks a config value at run time, and a plug-in can evade it by naming a field `x` (CXC-PC-PERMISSIONS `never-verified-scope`).
- rule: Result shape. `validate_manifest` returns the `Manifest` as before; it gains the read-only attribute `advisories`, a tuple of `Advisory(code, field, message)`, empty for a manifest that states "0.1" or "0.2". `ConsentContent` gains one line kind, `advisory`, after the permission lines.
- rule: Name tokens. a property name is lower-cased and split at `_`, `-`, `.`, spaces and at each lower-to-upper case boundary; a token is compared for exact equality, never as a substring. The tokens are: `namespace`, `topic`, `host`, `hostname`, `ip`, `url`, `uri`, `endpoint`, `path`, `dir`, `directory`, `folder`, `filename`, `filepath`, `grant`, `permission`, `permissions`. So `target_host` and `statePath` draw an advisory; `greeting`, `repeat`, `threshold` and `hostile` do not.
- rule: Formats. `hostname`, `idn-hostname`, `ipv4`, `ipv6`, `uri`, `uri-reference`, `iri`, `iri-reference`, `uri-template`.
- rule: Walk. the validator visits every schema reachable from the root through `properties`, `patternProperties`, `additionalProperties` and `items` when they hold a schema, `prefixItems`, `$defs`, `definitions`, `allOf`, `anyOf`, `oneOf`, `not`, `if`, `then` and `else`; it checks each property name it meets and each schema's `format`. A `patternProperties` key is a pattern, not a name, and is not tested.
- rule: Where. the advisories are computed wherever the manifest is validated (prepare, install, upgrade) and shown wherever consent content is rendered. `compose-plugin build` SHOULD print the same advisories for the generated `config_schema`, so the author learns at build time.
- consequence: because the lists only produce advisories, a change to them never changes whether a manifest is accepted; adding a token or a format after the freeze is therefore additive (CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN, CXC-PC-V03-RULING-GRANT-TOKENS).
- code-read: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/manifest.py lines 656 to 660: `if "config_schema" in manifest:` `v = manifest["config_schema"]` `if not isinstance(v, dict):` `refuse("config_schema", f"must be a JSON Schema object, got "` — the 0.2 validator checks only that `config_schema` is an object, and `refuse` is not called for any other reason.
- default: none; a manifest whose `config_schema` is the empty-object schema draws no advisory.
- boundary-case: `{"properties": {"target_host": {"type": "string"}}}` installs and draws one advisory naming `config_schema/properties/target_host` and the token `host`; `{"properties": {"site": {"type": "string", "format": "uri"}}}` draws one naming `config_schema/properties/site` and the format `uri`; a grant-bearing property inside `$defs` or under `items` draws one naming its full pointer; the same schema in a manifest stating "0.2" draws none.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-NC-GRANT-BEARING-CONFIG; the hello-world v1, v2 and EULA manifests draw no advisory.
- verify-human: an operator reading the consent page for a manifest with a `target_host` config field sees the advisory line, and the Accept control is unaffected.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-NC-GRANT-BEARING-CONFIG]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the raw output of the table-driven advisory test with the library commit and the sha256 of the output file
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-28 by composeux-architect, contract owner
- provenance: architect-synthesis — made ADVISORY, not a refusal, by the contract owner's ruling of 2026-11-28 under the rule that declared claims are never enforced; the token and format lists, the walk and the version gate are the writer's
