### [CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES] `capabilities` is an optional list of declared capability tokens; `agent-messaging` is the one defined now; a declaration is never enforced or verified

- rule: (1) `capabilities` is an optional manifest field, valid only in a manifest that states `contract: "0.3"` (CXC-PC-V03-VERSION-0-3). (2) Its value is a list of at most 16 strings; each matches `^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`; a duplicate is refused. (3) Defined now: `agent-messaging`, meaning the plug-in says it sends messages to agents or receives messages from agents. (4) A well-formed token that is not defined is ACCEPTED (CXC-PC-V03-RULING-UNKNOWN-CAPABILITY-TOKENS); only a defined token can trigger a host policy (CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK). Defining a further token is a new contract version. (5) The consent content carries one line `capabilities: <token>, <token> (declared by the plug-in; not verified)`, placed after the `permissions` lines and before the EULA block; when the field is absent there is no such line. (6) A capability is not a grant: it adds nothing to `granted`, so it never widens an upgrade; but a NEW token in an upgrade is a consent trigger of its own (CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE). (7) Declarations are never enforced or verified: no host code path reads `capabilities` except the validator, the consent renderer and the host policy hook, and no run-time check compares a plug-in's behaviour with it.
- default: absent means none declared, the same as `[]`.
- boundary-case: each is REFUSED at install naming `capabilities`: a value that is not a list, an entry that is not a string, an entry that breaks the grammar (`Agent-Messaging`, `agent_messaging`, the empty string), a duplicate, more than 16 entries, and the field in a manifest stating "0.1" or "0.2". ACCEPTED: `[]`, `["agent-messaging"]`, `["agent-messaging", "x-example"]`.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-T-CAPABILITIES-WARNINGS-TABLE.
