### [CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK] A host MAY add host-required warnings keyed on declared capabilities and MAY refuse an install or upgrade by policy, through one optional `Host.policy` member

- rule: (1) `Host` gains one OPTIONAL member, `policy(self, view) -> HostPolicy`. `view` is a read-only `PolicyView` holding `domain`, `version`, `capabilities` (a tuple, empty when none) and a deep copy of the manifest. `HostPolicy` holds `warnings` (a tuple of `{id, title, text}` under the grammar of CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS) and `refuse` (a one-line string or None). The base class returns `HostPolicy()`; a host written against 0.2 needs no change. (2) The library calls `policy` when it prepares an install or an upgrade (to render) and again, afresh, inside `install` and `upgrade` (to check what was acknowledged). The hook MUST be free of side effects. (3) Host warnings are shown on the same page as the plug-in's, after them, labelled "required by this host". (4) A non-None `refuse` stops the install or upgrade with the new `PolicyRefused` (a subclass of `PluginContractRefusal`) carrying the host's text; it is not an operator decision, so it writes no consent record and no lifecycle entry, and nothing changes. (5) A `policy` that raises fails closed: the operation is refused naming the exception CLASS only, nothing written. (6) The hook decides only what to show or whether to refuse; nothing it returns is verified against the plug-in's behaviour. (7) The text of any host warning (Gnomon's fixed text for `agent-messaging`) belongs to the host's own spec, not to this contract.
- default: no host warnings and no refusal.
- boundary-case: a host warning whose `id` or text breaks the grammar is a host defect: the operation is refused naming the id, nothing written; a host warning and a plug-in warning may share an `id` (their acknowledgement keys differ, CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK).
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-T-WARNING-ACK.
- verify-human: on Gnomon an operator installing a plug-in that declares `agent-messaging` sees the host's fixed warning beside the plug-in's own.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-T-WARNING-ACK]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the raw output of the hook test with the library commit and the sha256 of the output file
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-28 by composeux-architect, contract owner
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the hook, host warnings keyed on declared capabilities and refusal by policy are the contract owner's direction of 2026-11-28; the member's shape, its call points and the fail-closed reading are the writer's
- basis: owner-decided (project owner)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-LIBRARY-CONTENTS], [CXC-PC-UI-HOST-HOOKS], [CXC-PC-HOST-SECTIONS], [CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK]

### [CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK] Each warning, the plug-in's and the host's, needs its own acknowledgement, and the consent record stores the sha256 of each acknowledged text

- rule: (1) The consent page shows the EULA and every warning together on one page; each warning has its own control, a checkbox named `ack` whose value is the acknowledgement key `<source>:<id>` with `source` `plugin` or `host`. (2) Accept needs every control set; refuse and cancel need none. (3) `install` and `upgrade` gain an optional keyword `acknowledged_warnings`, a mapping from acknowledgement key to the sha256 shown, mirroring `consented_eula_sha256`. When the prompt carries any warning and a gate runs, the mapping MUST hold exactly the prompt's keys, each value equal to the lowercase hex sha256 of that warning's `text` as UTF-8 exactly as declared; otherwise the operation raises the new `WarningAckRefused` naming the key, before the gate, with nothing recorded and nothing placed. A mapping given when no warning exists is refused naming it. (4) The consent record gains ONE optional key, `acknowledged_warnings`: a list of `{id, sha256}` objects sorted by `id`, where `id` is the acknowledgement key; it is present only on a `granted` record whose prompt carried warnings, and omitted otherwise, never null or empty. A refused record omits it. (5) `granted` is unchanged: a warning is not a grant. (6) At an upgrade where no gate runs there is nothing to acknowledge and nothing is recorded; a NEW or CHANGED warning is itself a gate trigger, so such an upgrade always reaches rule (1) (CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE).
- code-read: python/compose_plugins/compose_plugins/consent.py line 369 `OPTIONAL_RECORD_KEYS = ("eula_sha256",)` and lines 487 to 493 refuse any key set outside the eight keys plus that one; lifecycle.py `_check_eula` (line 386) is the pattern: it refuses before the gate when the shown digest and the file's differ.
- default: no warning, no key, no acknowledgement.
- boundary-case: a mapping with a missing key, an extra key, a non-hex digest or a digest that differs from the warning's text is refused naming the key; a web accept with one box clear is refused naming the key and records nothing; the host's warning edited between prepare and install (a different digest) is refused naming it.
- verify-machine: CXC-PC-V03-T-WARNING-ACK.
- verify-human: on the glass an operator cannot press accept until each warning is acknowledged, and refuse works with every box clear.
- tested-by: [CXC-PC-V03-T-WARNING-ACK]
- evidence: EMPTY
- evidence-fills-with: the raw output of the acknowledgement test and a consent line carrying the key, with the library commit and the sha256 of each file
- status: ratified
- status-detail: ratified 2026-11-28 by composeux-architect, contract owner
- provenance: architect-synthesis — one page, one acknowledgement per warning and the sha256 of the warning text in the consent record are the contract owner's direction of 2026-11-28; the key name, the mapping argument and the refusal order follow the EULA pattern of CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HASH-RECORD
- basis: owner-decided (project owner)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HASH-RECORD], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-HOST-OBLIGATION], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNINGS], [CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK], [CXC-PC-V03-STORES-COMPAT]

