## 19. v0.3 open decisions for the architect's ratify

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-OPERATOR-NULL] Ruled: when a library caller supplies no `operator`, the entry records null

- question: every entry is "operator-attributed", but `operator` is optional on the library calls so that 0.2 callers keep working. Should a call with no operator be refused instead?
- lean: record null. The web manager always supplies the authenticated identity, so the attribution the contract owner wants holds wherever a person acts; a refusal would break every 0.2 programmatic caller, against the additive rule. A null is visible in the log.
- if-reopened: make `operator` a required keyword on `configure`, `enable`, `disable` and `uninstall`, refuse a blank one with `InstallLogRefused`, and drop the null from `operator` in CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD `new-keys`.
- invalidating-assumption: a host calls the library from an unattended path and still needs the log to name a person
- answered-by: ratified by the contract owner, 2026-11-28, on the lean below, which is now the rule
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD], [CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD-WRITE]

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-CONSENT-ID] Ruled: the consent-record id is the sha256 of the record's line, not a new key on the record

- question: the brief asks for the consent-record id in the entry, but a consent record has no id (it has eight keys, CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD). Add an `id` key to the record, or derive one?
- lean: derive it. A new key on every record would make every 0.3 consent line unreadable to a 0.2 library, the reason CXC-PC-V02-RULING-RECORD-KIND kept the record at eight keys. The digest of the exact line identifies the record without touching it, and the entry already embeds the record in `consent`.
- if-reopened: add an optional `id` to the record under CXC-PC-V03-STORES-COMPAT and have `make_record` generate it.
- invalidating-assumption: a host needs to cite a consent record by an id that survives re-serialisation of the line
- answered-by: ratified by the contract owner, 2026-11-28, on the lean below, which is now the rule
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD], [CXC-PC-CONSENT-RECORD], [CXC-PC-V02-RULING-RECORD-KIND]

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-LOG-KEYS] Ruled: the lifecycle entry reuses the 0.2 log and its key names instead of a second file with the brief's key names

- question: the brief names the fields `op`, `time`, `result`, `operator`; the 0.2 log already carries `event` and `at` and a strict key set. Reuse the 0.2 file and map the names, or write a new file?
- lean: reuse and map. Two logs of one lifecycle can disagree, and CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-DIVERGENCE already checks one against the snapshot; the cost is that a 0.2 reader skips 0.3 lines loudly (CXC-PC-V03-STORES-COMPAT `known-limit`).
- if-reopened: write the 0.3 entries to a second file, `lifecycle.jsonl`, with `op`, `domain`, `version`, `operator`, `time`, `result` and `consent_id`, and leave `installs.jsonl` as in 0.2.
- invalidating-assumption: a host must keep reading `installs.jsonl` with a 0.2 library after a 0.3 library has written to the same state root
- answered-by: ratified by the contract owner, 2026-11-28, on the lean below, which is now the rule
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD], [CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-FILE], [CXC-PC-V02-INSTALL-LOG-DIVERGENCE]

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-RECORD-DECLINE-UNCHANGED] Ruled: `record_decline` still writes one consent line and no lifecycle entry

- question: a decline is a lifecycle entry (CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD). `PluginManager.record_decline` also records an operator's refusal, but CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-ONE-RECORD-NOTHING-ELSE-CHANGES requires that it change nothing but one consent line. Should it now also append a `decline` entry?
- lean: no. The 0.2 contract and its test say "one record and nothing else"; the gate refusals and the no-gate decline already write entries; `record_decline` is the call a host makes when it ran its own screen, and the host may log that itself.
- if-reopened: add one `decline` entry to `record_decline` and amend CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE `writes` and the RD tests 1 and 4.
- invalidating-assumption: a host relies on the log alone to reconstruct every refusal
- answered-by: ratified by the contract owner, 2026-11-28, on the lean below, which is now the rule
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V02-RECORD-DECLINE], [CXC-PC-V02-T-RD-ONE-RECORD-NOTHING-ELSE-CHANGES], [CXC-PC-V03-LIFECYCLE-RECORD]

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-DATA-FOLDER] Ruled: the plug-in's data folder is `<state_root>/data/<domain>`, supplied by `Host.data_dir`

- question: the brief says "the plug-in's data folder" and "the exact data path inside the host state root", but the 0.2 library has no such folder. Where is it, and who names it?
- lean: `Host.data_dir(domain)`, default `<state_root>/data/<domain>`, an optional member so a host can place it elsewhere inside the state root; the preflight refuses anything that is not strictly inside.
- if-reopened: fix the path in the contract with no host member, or name a different directory.
- invalidating-assumption: a host already keeps plug-in data in a location the default does not match
- answered-by: ratified by the contract owner, 2026-11-28, on the lean below, which is now the rule
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-UNINSTALL-DATA-CHOICE]

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-GRANT-TOKENS] Ruled: grant-bearing is a closed list of name tokens and schema formats that produces advisories only, so changing it never narrows the frozen manifest

- question: "grant-bearing" needs a mechanical test, and the audit asked whether a change to the lists after 0.3 would be a MAJOR under CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN rule (3).
- rule: the token and format lists of CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG, applied to manifests stating "0.3" only, drive an ADVISORY (a named warning), never a refusal. A list change after 0.3 changes which advisories appear and never whether a manifest is accepted, so it is ADDITIVE and may ship in a MINOR. It is a structural author aid, not enforcement.
- if-reopened: if a later contract makes the screen refuse, that is a narrowing of a frozen field and is a MAJOR.
- invalidating-assumption: authors name grant-bearing fields in ways the lists miss, or ordinary fields collide with them often enough that the advisory is ignored
- answered-by: ratified by the contract owner, 2026-11-28 (a closed list, a screen, not proof); made advisory by the ruling of the same date
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-GRANT-FREE-CONFIG], [CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN]

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-DECLARATION-CHANGE-GATES] Ruled: a new or changed capability or warning in an upgrade IS a consent-gate trigger, like a changed EULA

- question: the first draft followed the brief's wording that the gate runs exactly at install, widening and EULA change, so a warning or capability added in an upgrade would not be shown. Should it be a trigger?
- rule: YES. A NEW or CHANGED `capabilities` token, or a NEW or CHANGED warning (by text sha256), in an upgrade is a trigger, because it changes the risk the operator accepted; `agent-messaging` arriving in an upgrade must not slip through. Removals are not triggers.
- applied-in: CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS, CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE, CXC-PC-V03-T-UPGRADE-ADDS-AGENT-MESSAGING, CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES, CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK
- if-reopened: drop the third trigger from CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS rule (1) and delete the predicate node and its test.
- invalidating-assumption: operators are asked so often by host warning-text edits that they stop reading the page
- answered-by: ratified by the contract owner, 2026-11-28 (reverses the draft's lean)
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-CONSENT-TRIGGERS], [CXC-PC-V03-DECLARATION-CHANGE-PREDICATE], [CXC-PC-V03-WARNING-ACK], [CXC-PC-V02-EULA-RECONSENT]

### [CXC-PC-V03-RULING-UNKNOWN-CAPABILITY-TOKENS] Ruled: a well-formed capability token that is not defined is accepted and shown, now and in every later contract

- question: only `agent-messaging` is defined. Should an author's other token be refused? The audit noted that refusing later would narrow `capabilities` and breach the freeze.
- rule: ACCEPT, and state it so it only adds. A well-formed undefined token is accepted and shown as a claim, and no later contract refuses it; a later contract may DEFINE a meaning for a token (additive: a host policy or a consent line), never refuse one that 0.3 accepted. An accepted undefined token does nothing but appear.
- if-reopened: refusing undefined tokens is a MAJOR (it narrows a frozen field) and is outside this contract line.
- invalidating-assumption: a plug-in uses an undefined token to dodge a host's policy by a near-miss spelling
- answered-by: resolved by the writer on the contract owner's instruction of 2026-11-28 to close it additively
- status: decided
- basis: contract-owner design call
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]
- see-also: [CXC-PC-V03-CAPABILITIES], [CXC-PC-V03-HOST-POLICY-HOOK], [CXC-PC-V03-MANIFEST-FROZEN]

